QB.add(43,[{"id":43001,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"A coronary artery is blocked for 20 minutes and then reopened. Which pair of terms describes the two phases of injury to the heart muscle?","o":["Ischaemia then reperfusion","Hypertrophy then hyperplasia of the cardiomyocytes","Metaplasia then atrophy of the heart wall","Autophagy then differentiation of the stem cells","Apoptosis then proliferation of the cardiomyocytes"],"e":"Ischaemia is restriction of blood flow leading to oxygen starvation. Reperfusion is the return of oxygenated blood flow after a period of restriction, which itself causes cell stress and inflammation. Oxygen starvation (ischaemia-reperfusion) is one of the listed causes of cell injury.","w":["Hypertrophy and hyperplasia are adaptations, not the phases of blood flow injury.","Metaplasia and atrophy are adaptations.","Autophagy and differentiation do not describe this event.","Cardiomyocytes are permanent cells and do not proliferate."],"k":"Ischaemia = restricted flow; reperfusion = return of flow (also injurious).","rn":[["","What is meant by Ischaemia?","What is meant by Ischaemia?","Restriction of blood flow; Leads to oxygen starvation"],["","Reperfusion","Reperfusion","Oxygenated blood returns; After restriction"]]},{"id":43002,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a cause of cell injury?","o":["Adequate growth factor stimulation","Trauma","Osmotic pressure changes","DNA damage from radiation or free radicals","Drugs, poisons or toxins"],"e":"Causes of cell injury: trauma, osmotic pressure changes, thermal injury, oxygen starvation (ischaemia-reperfusion), infection, membrane failure, DNA damage (radiation, chemotherapy, free radicals), metabolic disruption, LACK of growth factors, and drugs/poisons/toxins.","w":["Trauma is listed.","Osmotic changes are listed.","DNA damage is listed.","Drugs, poisons and toxins are listed."],"k":"Lack (not presence) of growth factors injures cells.","rn":[["","Cell injury: 3 other causes?","Cell injury: 3 other causes?","Infection; Lack of growth factors; Drugs, poisons, toxins"],["","What drives pathologic hyperplasia?","What drives pathologic hyperplasia?","More hormone or growth factor; Stimulation"]]},{"id":43003,"t":"Cell Injury","a":["reperfusion","reperfusion injury"],"s":"What term describes the return of oxygenated blood flow after a period of restriction, which itself causes cell stress and inflammation?","e":"Reperfusion is the return of oxygenated blood flow after a period of restriction (ischaemia); it causes cell stress and inflammation.","k":"Reperfusion can injure cells.","rn":[["","Reperfusion","Reperfusion","Oxygenated blood returns; After restriction"]]},{"id":43004,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"Which sequence correctly describes how cell injury becomes disease?","o":["Cell damage → tissue damage → organ dysfunction → system dysfunction → symptoms","Symptoms → system dysfunction → organ damage → tissue damage → cell damage","Organ damage → cell damage → symptoms → tissue damage","Cell damage → symptoms → tissue damage → organ damage","Tissue damage → cell damage → system dysfunction → symptoms"],"e":"Damage to individual cells collectively presents as tissue damage, then organ damage (limited or disrupted function), then disruption of system function, then pathology symptoms. Injury never hits just one cell: a group of cells is injured.","w":["This reverses the sequence.","Cell damage precedes organ damage.","Symptoms come last.","Cell damage comes first."],"k":"Cells → tissue → organ → system → symptoms.","rn":[["","Injury progression: last 2 levels?","Injury progression: last 2 levels?","System disruption; Pathology symptoms"],["","Injury progression: first 3 levels?","Injury progression: first 3 levels?","Individual cell; Tissue; Organ"]]},{"id":43005,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"What are the possible fates of an injured cell?","o":["Survival if reversible; apoptosis or necrosis if irreversible","Only necrosis in every case of injury, regardless of severity","Only apoptosis in every case, because death is always programmed","Conversion into a stem cell that can regenerate the tissue","Immediate division to replace itself with two healthy cells"],"e":"A normal cell in homeostasis suffers cell injury, which is either reversible (the cell survives) or irreversible (the cell dies by apoptosis or necrosis). Apoptosis is organised cell death; necrosis is disorganised.","w":["Injury can be reversible or end in apoptosis.","Necrosis also occurs.","Differentiated cells do not revert to stem cells.","Injury does not trigger immediate replacement division."],"k":"Reversible → survive; irreversible → apoptosis or necrosis.","rn":[["","What are the 2 possible outcomes of irreversible cell injury?","What are the 2 possible outcomes of irreversible cell injury?","Apoptosis; Necrosis"],["","Image card: Kidney epithelium after blood flow restriction: reversible or irreversible injury? How …","Kidney epithelium after blood flow restriction: reversible or irreversible injury? How can you tell?","Irreversible (necrosis); Cells lack nuclei"],["","What happens to a cell after reversible injury?","What happens to a cell after reversible injury?","Survival"]]},{"id":43006,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"Which pairing of trigger and typical death route is consistent with the lecture?","o":["DNA damage → apoptosis","Oxygen starvation → apoptosis only","Mutations → necrosis only","Oxidative stress → apoptosis only","Radiation → necrosis only"],"e":"Apoptosis is typically triggered by mutations, DNA damage and infection/inflammation. Necrosis is typically triggered by oxygen starvation, oxidative stress and infection/inflammation. DNA damage often leads to programmed cell death, so the event is contained.","w":["Oxygen starvation typically leads to necrosis.","Mutations typically lead to apoptosis.","Oxidative stress typically leads to necrosis.","Radiation most likely leads to apoptosis."],"k":"DNA damage/mutation → apoptosis; O<sub>2</sub> starvation/oxidative stress → necrosis.","f":"ci06","rn":[["","Which 3 causes lead to apoptosis?","Which 3 causes lead to apoptosis?","Mutations; DNA damage; Infection/inflammation"]]},{"id":43007,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"Why is apoptosis generally a better outcome for the body than necrosis?","o":["It is contained and causes no inflammation","It always lets the dying cell recover","It spreads damage widely to the neighbouring tissue","It leaks the cell's enzymes into the surrounding tissue","It triggers sepsis to clear the remaining infection"],"e":"Apoptosis is organised: the membrane stays intact, contents do not leak, and there is no inflammatory stimulus, so the event is contained. Necrosis is disorganised: the membrane falls apart, contents leak and inflammation follows, with a worse outcome for the body.","w":["Apoptosis is death, not recovery.","Apoptosis is contained, not widespread.","Leakage of contents is necrosis.","Sepsis is a possible consequence of inflammation from necrosis."],"k":"Apoptosis: contained, no inflammation.","rn":[["","Which 3 causes lead to apoptosis?","Which 3 causes lead to apoptosis?","Mutations; DNA damage; Infection/inflammation"],["","Which 3 causes lead to necrosis?","Which 3 causes lead to necrosis?","Oxygen starvation; Oxidative stress; Infection/inflammation"]]},{"id":43008,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"Kidney tubular cells swell after a short period of reduced blood flow. Why do injured cells swell?","o":["Energy-dependent membrane ion pumps fail","The nucleus divides rapidly inside the cell","The cell membrane becomes stronger and thicker","Caspases are activated within the cytoplasm","The cells convert to squamous epithelium"],"e":"Swelling is the first sign of injury: the energy-dependent cell membrane ion pumps fail, so cells take in water. Other signs include lipid vacuoles, increased eosin staining, membrane blebs, swollen mitochondria and a condensing nucleus.","w":["Rapid nuclear division is not a sign of injury.","The membrane becomes distorted, not stronger.","Caspase activation marks irreversible apoptosis.","Squamous change is metaplasia, an adaptation."],"k":"Swelling = failure of energy-dependent ion pumps.","rn":[["","Why do cells swell in reversible injury?","Why do cells swell in reversible injury?","Failure of membrane ion pumps; Pumps are energy dependent; Cells take in water"]]},{"id":43009,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Which of the following is NOT a sign of reversible cell injury listed in the lecture?","o":["Formation of apoptotic bodies","Swelling of the cell","Lipid vacuoles in the cytoplasm","Membrane blebs and distortion","Swelling of the mitochondria"],"e":"Signs of injury in order: swelling (pump failure), lipid vacuoles, increased eosinophil staining, membrane blebs and distortion, swollen mitochondria, and a condensing nucleus. Apoptotic bodies form only once the cell has entered programmed death, which is irreversible.","w":["Swelling is listed.","Lipid vacuoles are listed.","Blebs and distortion are listed.","Mitochondrial swelling is listed."],"k":"Apoptotic bodies = irreversible death, not reversible injury.","rn":[["","Apoptotic bodies","Apoptotic bodies","Smaller fragments; Of the apoptotic cell"],["","What marks apoptotic bodies for phagocytes?","What marks apoptotic bodies for phagocytes?","Ligands for phagocytic cell receptors"]]},{"id":43010,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"When does reversible injury become irreversible, according to the lecture?","o":["Once cell death mechanisms begin","As soon as the cell swells even slightly","Only after the patient develops clinical symptoms","Only when the cell divides again","Never; all injury is reversible if treated"],"e":"Reversible injury means cells can return to normal if the cause is removed in good time. Cells are resilient up to a point; once cell death mechanisms begin, the injury has become irreversible.","w":["Swelling is an early, reversible sign.","Symptoms are not the cellular threshold.","Division is not the threshold.","Injury can become irreversible."],"k":"Death mechanisms started = irreversible.","rn":[["","When does cell injury become irreversible?","When does cell injury become irreversible?","Once cell death mechanisms begin"]]},{"id":43011,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"In these kidney epithelium panels, how can you tell a cell has died?","o":["No nucleus can be seen","It is neat and cuboidal","It has many mitochondria","It stains more blue","It has divided into two"],"e":"Normal kidney epithelium is cuboidal and tidy. After blood flow restriction, cells look messy with distorted borders, and some show a condensing nucleus. A cell where you cannot see a nucleus is dead. If the stressor is removed, cells not yet dead may recover.","w":["Neat cuboidal cells are normal.","Mitochondria count is not the sign used.","Injury increases eosin (pink) staining.","Division does not indicate death."],"k":"No visible nucleus = dead cell.","f":"ci07","rn":[["","How can you tell a kidney epithelial cell has died?","How can you tell a kidney epithelial cell has died?","No visible nucleus"]]},{"id":43012,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"In reversible injury, the cells stain more strongly with which component of H&amp;E?","o":["Eosin (red/pink)","Haematoxylin (blue)","Neither","Both equally","Only a silver stain"],"e":"Increased eosinophil (red) staining in histopathology is one of the signs of injury. Coagulative necrosis also stains strongly with eosin because dead cells lose their nuclei, leaving protein remnants.","w":["Increased red, not blue, staining is described.","Staining changes are described.","Eosin staining increases.","H&amp;E is used."],"k":"Injured cells: increased eosin staining."},{"id":43013,"t":"Cell Growth","s":"Which statement about cell proliferation is consistent with the lecture?","o":["Stem and progenitor cells keep the ability; differentiated cells lose it","All adult cells keep proliferating at the same high rate as cells in the developing embryo","Proliferation stops completely after birth","Differentiated cells proliferate more than stem cells","Proliferation never happens after injury"],"e":"Cells proliferate through the cell cycle in response to stimuli such as mitogens, growth proteins and survival proteins. Proliferation peaks in embryonic development and varies with cell type in adults; it can occur after injury for repair. Stem and progenitor cells keep the ability; differentiated cells lose it.","w":["Adult rates vary by cell type and are lower than embryonic.","Some adult cells still proliferate.","Stem cells retain proliferation.","Repair involves proliferation."],"k":"Stem/progenitor cells proliferate; differentiated cells don't.","rn":[["","Which cells retain the ability to proliferate?","Which cells retain the ability to proliferate?","Stem cells; Progenitor cells"],["","Which cells proliferate in hyperplasia?","Which cells proliferate in hyperplasia?","Differentiated cells; Progenitor cells"],["","What lies between a stem cell and a mature cell?","What lies between a stem cell and a mature cell?","Intermediate differentiated progenitor cells"]]},{"id":43014,"t":"Cell Growth","a":["mitogen","mitogens"],"s":"What term describes a signal that induces mitosis?","e":"Mitogens are signalling molecules that induce mitosis; along with growth proteins and survival proteins, they trigger proliferation.","k":"Mitogen = mitosis-inducing signal.","rn":[["","What do mitogens do?","What do mitogens do?","Induce mitosis"],["","What are the 3 types of stimuli that trigger cell proliferation?","What are the 3 types of stimuli that trigger cell proliferation?","Mitogens; Growth proteins; Survival proteins"]]},{"id":43015,"t":"Cell Growth","s":"Which pairing of cell cycle phase and event is correct?","o":["S phase: DNA is replicated","G1: nuclear division","M phase: DNA is replicated","G2: cell division by cytokinesis","G0: the cell divides repeatedly"],"e":"G1 (gap 1): the cell grows; non-dividing cells are usually arrested here. S: DNA synthesis. G2: second gap before division. M: mitosis (nuclear division), then cytokinesis. G0: terminally differentiated cells stay in an extended G1 and do not progress.","w":["G1 is growth; nuclear division is M.","DNA replication is S.","Cytokinesis is at the end of M.","G0 is a resting state."],"k":"G1 grow; S copy DNA; G2 gap; M divide; G0 rest.","rn":[["","What happens in S phase?","What happens in S phase?","DNA synthesis"]]},{"id":43016,"t":"Cell Growth","s":"What is G0?","o":["A resting state of terminally differentiated cells","The phase of the cycle in which the cell's DNA is replicated before division","The phase of nuclear division","The phase of cytokinesis","A phase seen only in cancer cells"],"e":"Terminally differentiated cells stay at G1 and do not progress; this extended resting phase is called G0. Most cells in the body are in G0, and for some cell types this is reversible (e.g. liver cells after damage).","w":["DNA replication is S phase.","Nuclear division is M phase.","Cytokinesis ends M phase.","Most body cells are in G0."],"k":"G0 = resting, non-dividing state.","rn":[["","G0","G0","Extended G1; Terminally differentiated cells; Cycle no longer progresses"]]},{"id":43017,"t":"Cell Growth","s":"On this cell cycle diagram, what makes up interphase?","o":["G1, S and G2","M phase only","G0 and M","S and M","Cytokinesis alone"],"e":"The cell cycle runs G1 (with the restriction point R), S (DNA synthesis), G2 and M (mitosis, then cytokinesis). Interphase is G1 + S + G2. Cells that do not divide are usually arrested in G1.","w":["M phase is division, not interphase.","G0 is outside the cycle; M is division.","M is not part of interphase.","Cytokinesis ends M phase."],"k":"Interphase = G1 + S + G2.","f":"ci10"},{"id":43018,"t":"Tissue Organisation","a":["g0","g zero","gap 0","g0 phase"],"s":"What is the name of the resting phase in which terminally differentiated cells stay without dividing?","e":"G0 is the resting phase: terminally differentiated cells stay in an extended G1 and do not progress through the cycle.","k":"G0 = resting phase.","rn":[["","Most cells in the body are in which phase?","Most cells in the body are in which phase?","G0 (non-dividing)"]]},{"id":43019,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"Differentiated cells are taken from a tissue and grown in culture. What does the lecturer say happens?","o":["They divide a couple of times at most, then die out","They keep dividing indefinitely as long as nutrients are supplied","They turn into stem cells","They become cancer cells","They never die"],"e":"If you take differentiated cells from a tissue and grow them in culture, they divide a couple of times at most, then die out. Many human cells can divide only a finite number of times.","w":["They do not divide indefinitely.","Differentiation is one-way.","This is not described.","They die out."],"k":"Differentiated cells have limited division capacity.","rn":[["","How many times can many human cells divide?","How many times can many human cells divide?","A finite number"]]},{"id":43020,"t":"Cell Growth","s":"Which statement about differentiation is correct?","o":["It is one-way: a progenitor cannot revert to a stem cell","Differentiated cells can freely become stem cells again","Differentiation and division never happen together","Only neurons can differentiate","Differentiation uses up stem cells without replacing them"],"e":"The order is stem cell, progenitor cell, intermediate stages, final cell, and it is one-way: a progenitor cannot go back to a stem cell. Division and differentiation happen together, otherwise the stem/progenitor pool would be used up.","w":["Differentiation is one-way.","They are coupled.","Many cell types differentiate.","Coupling with division maintains the pool."],"k":"Differentiation is one-way and coupled with division.","rn":[["","Can a progenitor cell go back to being a stem cell?","Can a progenitor cell go back to being a stem cell?","No, it is locked"],["","Cell differentiation","Cell differentiation","Stem/progenitor cells; Become more specific type"],["","What lies between a stem cell and a mature cell?","What lies between a stem cell and a mature cell?","Intermediate differentiated progenitor cells"]]},{"id":43021,"t":"Cell Growth","s":"What determines how well a tissue can self-renew and repair after injury?","o":["Its resident stem or progenitor cells","The colour of the tissue under the microscope","The number of neurons in the organ","The amount of extracellular fat","How old the patient's parents are"],"e":"Self-renewal and repair are not the same in all tissues: they depend on resident stem or progenitor cells, which are kept quiescent until they receive molecular signals to enter the cell cycle and divide.","w":["Colour does not determine repair.","Neurons are permanent cells.","Fat is not the determinant.","Parental age is irrelevant."],"k":"Repair depends on resident stem/progenitor cells.","rn":[["","What does a tissue's repair capability depend on?","What does a tissue's repair capability depend on?","Resident stem or progenitor cells"]]},{"id":43022,"t":"Cell Growth","a":["quiescent","quiescence"],"s":"What term describes stem or progenitor cells kept resting until signalled to divide?","e":"Resident stem or progenitor cells are kept quiescent (resting) until needed, when molecular signals make them enter the cell cycle and divide.","k":"Quiescent = resting until signalled.","rn":[["","What makes a quiescent stem cell enter the cell cycle and divide?","What makes a quiescent stem cell enter the cell cycle and divide?","Molecular signals"],["","What state are resident stem cells kept in until needed?","What state are resident stem cells kept in until needed?","Quiescent state"]]},{"id":43023,"t":"Cell Growth","s":"Where are the \"true\" stem cells found, according to the lecturer?","o":["The inner cell mass of the blastocyst","The adult liver","Mature red blood cells","Cardiac muscle","The epidermis only"],"e":"The true stem cells are the embryonic stem cells of the inner cell mass of the blastocyst, the starting point of every cell in the body. Small pools of cells keep this ability in the adult (adult stem cells, which some argue are really progenitor cells).","w":["Hepatocytes are stable, not true stem cells.","Red cells are permanent.","Cardiac muscle is permanent.","Skin has stem cells but not the \"true\" embryonic ones."],"k":"True stem cells: inner cell mass of the blastocyst.","rn":[["","Where are true (embryonic) stem cells?","Where are true (embryonic) stem cells?","Inner cell mass; Of the blastocyst"]]},{"id":43024,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"A patient's cells are exposed to extreme cold. Which listed cause of cell injury applies?","o":["Thermal injury","Osmotic pressure changes","Lack of growth factors","Membrane failure","Infection"],"e":"Thermal injury (extreme hot or cold) is one of the listed causes of cell injury, alongside trauma, osmotic pressure changes, oxygen starvation, infection, membrane failure, DNA damage, metabolic disruption, lack of growth factors and drugs/poisons/toxins.","w":["Osmotic change is a separate cause.","Lack of growth factors is a separate cause.","Membrane failure is a separate cause.","Infection is a separate cause."],"k":"Extreme heat or cold = thermal injury.","rn":[["","Cell injury: 2 physical causes?","Cell injury: 2 physical causes?","Trauma; Thermal injury"]]},{"id":43025,"t":"Cell Injury","a":["ischaemia","ischemia"],"s":"What term describes restriction of blood flow leading to oxygen starvation?","e":"Ischaemia is restriction of blood flow leading to oxygen starvation. It reduces ATP, so energy-dependent functions fail, typically leading to necrosis.","k":"Ischaemia = restricted flow.","rn":[["","What is meant by Ischaemia?","What is meant by Ischaemia?","Restriction of blood flow; Leads to oxygen starvation"]]},{"id":43026,"t":"Cell Injury","a":["homeostasis"],"s":"What term describes the normal, balanced state of a cell before injury?","e":"A normal cell is in homeostasis. Injury pushes it out of this state, after which it may recover, adapt or die.","k":"Homeostasis = normal balanced state."},{"id":43027,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"Why is the epithelium more likely to proliferate after damage than muscle?","o":["Epithelial cells are more exposed to damage and have more proliferative capacity","Muscle cells are always dividing faster than epithelium, so muscle repairs first","Epithelium has no stem cells","Muscle tissue is never injured","Epithelium is made of permanent cells"],"e":"The lecturer noted that the epithelium is more likely to be damaged because of where it is and what it does, and its cells are more likely to proliferate than, for example, muscle cells. How well a tissue's cells can grow decides how likely the tissue is to be saved after injury.","w":["Muscle cells are largely permanent.","Labile epithelium contains stem cell pools.","Muscle can be injured.","Epithelium is labile."],"k":"Epithelium: exposed and highly proliferative.","rn":[["","What does cell recovery after injury depend on?","What does cell recovery after injury depend on?","Type of injury; Proliferative capacity; Differentiation capacity"],["","Why do epithelial cells proliferate more than muscle?","Why do epithelial cells proliferate more than muscle?","Epithelium is more damaged"]]},{"id":43028,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"In the kidney example, what happens if the stressor is removed in time?","o":["Cells not yet dead may recover, more or less saving the kidney","Every cell dies regardless","All cells become squamous","The kidney converts to liver tissue","The cells start dividing indefinitely"],"e":"If the stressor (restricted blood flow) is removed, the cells that are not yet dead may recover and the kidney is more or less saved. If not, most cells die. Cells are resilient up to a point.","w":["Not all cells die if the stressor is removed in time.","Metaplasia is not described here.","Tissues do not convert organs.","Uncontrolled division is not described."],"k":"Early removal of the stressor rescues reversibly injured cells.","rn":[["","What can save a kidney with distressed (not yet dead) cells?","What can save a kidney with distressed (not yet dead) cells?","Removing the stressor"]]},{"id":43029,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"Which change in the nucleus is listed as a sign of injury?","o":["The nucleus condenses","The nucleus doubles in size and divides","The nucleus moves outside the cell","The nucleus turns green","The nucleus becomes a mitochondrion"],"e":"Signs of injury include swelling, lipid vacuoles, increased eosin staining, membrane blebs, swollen mitochondria and a condensing nucleus. A cell with no visible nucleus is dead.","w":["Doubling and division are not injury signs.","The nucleus does not leave the cell.","Colour change to green is not described.","Organelles do not convert."],"k":"Injury: condensing nucleus.","rn":[["","Reversible injury: 3 other signs?","Reversible injury: 3 other signs?","Membrane blebs; Mitochondrial swelling; Condensed nucleus"]]},{"id":43030,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"Why is understanding cell regeneration important, according to the lecture?","o":["It matters for treating disease, transplantation, growing organs and neurodegeneration","It only matters for cosmetic surgery","It is purely of historical interest","It prevents all infections","It replaces the need for stem cells"],"e":"Understanding regeneration matters for treating disease, transplantation, growing tissues and organs, and neurodegeneration. A big research question is how to make our bodies regenerate when needed, with stem and progenitor cells central to it.","w":["Its relevance is much wider.","It is an active research field.","It does not prevent infections.","Stem cells are central to regeneration."],"k":"Regeneration research: disease, transplantation, organs, neurodegeneration.","rn":[["","Why is understanding tissue regeneration important?","Why is understanding tissue regeneration important?","Treating and managing disease; Transplantation and neurodegeneration"]]},{"id":43031,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Which feature distinguishes apoptosis from necrosis?","o":["The membrane stays intact and folds the cell into apoptotic bodies for phagocytes","The membrane falls apart and the cell's contents leak into the tissue","It always triggers a strong inflammatory response in the tissue","It is always pathological and never part of normal development","It is caused only by metabolic failure of the cell"],"e":"In apoptosis (programmed cell death) the membrane stays intact and helps fragment the cell into apoptotic bodies, which phagocytes degrade. It is tightly regulated, with no leakage of contents and so no inflammatory stimulus, and it is a physiological process in development.","w":["Membrane breakdown and leakage describe necrosis.","Apoptosis causes no inflammation.","Apoptosis is physiological in development.","Metabolic failure causes necrosis."],"k":"Apoptosis: intact membrane, apoptotic bodies, no inflammation.","rn":[["","What marks apoptotic bodies for phagocytes?","What marks apoptotic bodies for phagocytes?","Ligands for phagocytic cell receptors"],["","Apoptotic bodies","Apoptotic bodies","Smaller fragments; Of the apoptotic cell"],["","What happens to the cell membrane in apoptosis?","What happens to the cell membrane in apoptosis?","Remains intact; Helps fragment the cell"]]},{"id":43032,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Put the steps of apoptosis in order: (P) phagocytosis of fragments; (Q) condensation of chromatin and membrane blebs; (R) fragmentation into apoptotic bodies; (S) normal cell.","o":["S → Q → R → P","S → R → Q → P","Q → S → R → P","S → P → Q → R","R → Q → S → P"],"e":"Apoptosis: a normal cell; condensation of chromatin with membrane blebs; cellular fragmentation into apoptotic bodies; phagocytosis of the apoptotic cell and fragments by a phagocyte.","w":["Chromatin condenses before fragmentation.","The normal cell comes first.","Phagocytosis is last.","The cell condenses before fragmenting."],"k":"Normal → condense/bleb → fragment → phagocytosis.","rn":[["","Apoptosis: last 2 stages?","Apoptosis: last 2 stages?","Fragmentation; Phagocytosis of fragments"]]},{"id":43033,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Which of these is an example of physiological apoptosis given in the speaker notes?","o":["Separation of the digits as cells between them die","Pus forming in the meninges in bacterial meningitis","Fat necrosis in acute pancreatitis","Coagulative necrosis in a myocardial infarct","Caseous necrosis in tuberculosis of the lung"],"e":"Hands and feet start as spade-like structures, and the digits separate only as the cells between them die by apoptosis. Other examples: more than half of many nerve cell types die soon after forming; a tadpole's tail disappears; self-reactive or non-functional lymphocytes are removed.","w":["Pus formation reflects inflammation, not apoptosis.","Fat necrosis is necrosis.","Coagulative necrosis is necrosis.","Caseous necrosis is necrosis."],"k":"Digit separation = physiological apoptosis.","rn":[["","How does apoptosis shape hands and feet?","How does apoptosis shape hands and feet?","Cells between digits die; Digits separate from spade-like structure"]]},{"id":43034,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Developing B and T lymphocytes that make self-reactive receptors are removed. Which role of apoptosis does this illustrate?","o":["Quality control removing dangerous cells","Supplying nutrients through self-eating","Making cells bigger to cope with load","Replacing columnar with squamous cells","Causing inflammation to fight infection"],"e":"Apoptosis acts as quality control, removing abnormal, misplaced, non-functional or dangerous cells, such as developing T and B lymphocytes that fail to make useful antigen receptors or make self-reactive receptors.","w":["Self-eating for nutrients is autophagy.","Bigger cells is hypertrophy.","Cell-type replacement is metaplasia.","Apoptosis does not cause inflammation."],"k":"Apoptosis removes self-reactive lymphocytes (quality control).","rn":[["","Besides excess cells, which cells does apoptosis remove in development?","Besides excess cells, which cells does apoptosis remove in development?","Abnormal cells; Misplaced cells; Nonfunctional or dangerous cells"]]},{"id":43035,"t":"Cell Death","s":"A cell detects DNA damage it cannot repair. Why does it undergo apoptosis?","o":["To prevent cancer-promoting mutations and avoid necrosis","To trigger inflammation that repairs the DNA","To become a stem cell","To divide faster","To store more nutrients"],"e":"Cells that detect damage, especially DNA damage they cannot repair (which could produce cancer-promoting mutations), kill themselves by apoptosis. The lecturer added that if injury continued it could end in necrosis, which causes inflammation and is worse for the tissue; apoptosis protects against that.","w":["Apoptosis does not cause inflammation.","Differentiated cells do not revert to stem cells.","It dies rather than divides.","Nutrient storage is not the purpose."],"k":"Irreparable DNA damage → protective apoptosis.","rn":[["","Why do cells with unrepaired DNA damage undergo apoptosis?","Why do cells with unrepaired DNA damage undergo apoptosis?","Avoid cancer-promoting mutations"]]},{"id":43036,"t":"Cell Death","s":"More nerve cells are made in development than target cells can support. What happens to those that get too little survival factor?","o":["They undergo apoptosis","They undergo necrosis with inflammation","They become hypertrophic","They undergo metaplasia","They divide to compete"],"e":"More nerve cells are made than the target cells' limited survival factors can support; those that receive too little undergo apoptosis. This overproduction then culling ensures every target cell is contacted and the extra nerve cells are removed.","w":["Developmental culling is non-inflammatory apoptosis.","Hypertrophy is an adaptation.","Metaplasia is cell-type replacement.","Neurons are permanent and do not divide."],"k":"Survival factor shortage → neuronal apoptosis.","rn":[["","How do survival factors set nerve cell numbers?","How do survival factors set nerve cell numbers?","More made than supported; Too little factor: apoptosis"]]},{"id":43037,"t":"Cell Death","a":["apoptotic bodies","apoptotic body"],"s":"What are the membrane-bound fragments of an apoptotic cell, later eaten by phagocytes, called?","e":"In apoptosis the intact membrane helps fragment the cell into apoptotic bodies, which are degraded by phagocytes without inflammation.","k":"Apoptotic bodies are eaten by phagocytes.","rn":[["","Apoptotic bodies","Apoptotic bodies","Smaller fragments; Of the apoptotic cell"],["","What marks apoptotic bodies for phagocytes?","What marks apoptotic bodies for phagocytes?","Ligands for phagocytic cell receptors"]]},{"id":43038,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Which family of proteins carries out apoptosis by cleaving other proteins?","o":["Caspases","Cytokines","Mitogens","Lipases","Immunoglobulins"],"e":"Apoptosis is triggered by a family of proteins called caspases: specialised proteases that cleave other proteins to carry out programmed cell death. Once caspases are active there is no stopping it.","w":["Cytokines are signalling molecules.","Mitogens induce mitosis.","Lipases digest fat (relevant to fat necrosis).","Immunoglobulins are antibodies."],"k":"Caspases execute apoptosis.","rn":[["","What family of proteins triggers apoptosis?","What family of proteins triggers apoptosis?","Caspases"],["","Caspases","Caspases","Specialised proteases; Cleave proteins for apoptosis"]]},{"id":43039,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Which statement about the intrinsic pathway of apoptosis is correct?","o":["It relies on mitochondria releasing cytochrome c","It depends on TNF-family death receptors on the cell surface","It is responsible for only a tiny minority of apoptosis","It never activates caspases at any stage","It occurs only in the developing nervous system"],"e":"The intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway is responsible for most physiological and pathological apoptosis and relies on mitochondria releasing cytochrome c into the cytoplasm. The extrinsic pathway depends on death receptors, mostly of the TNF receptor family. Both converge on caspase activation.","w":["Death receptors belong to the extrinsic pathway.","It accounts for most apoptosis.","It activates caspases.","The nervous system example is extrinsic."],"k":"Intrinsic: mitochondria release cytochrome c.","rn":[["","What does the intrinsic pathway rely on?","What does the intrinsic pathway rely on?","Mitochondria; Cytochrome C release into cytoplasm"]]},{"id":43040,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Put the intrinsic pathway in order: (P) caspase activation; (Q) BH3 sensors detect injury; (R) Bcl-2 family effectors (Bax, Bak) activate; (S) mitochondria release cytochrome c.","o":["Q → R → S → P","R → Q → S → P","S → Q → R → P","Q → S → R → P","P → Q → R → S"],"e":"Cell injury (growth factor withdrawal, DNA damage, protein misfolding) is detected by BH3 sensors; Bcl-2 family effectors (Bax, Bak) are activated, opposed by regulators (Bcl-2, Bcl-xL); mitochondria release cytochrome c; caspases are activated, followed by endonuclease activation and cytoskeleton breakdown.","w":["Sensors detect injury first.","Cytochrome c release follows Bcl-2 effector activation.","Effectors act before cytochrome c release.","Caspase activation comes last of these."],"k":"BH3 sensors → Bax/Bak → cytochrome c → caspases.","rn":[["","What do BH3 sensors activate?","What do BH3 sensors activate?","Bcl-2 effectors (Bax, Bak); Release cytochrome C"]]},{"id":43041,"t":"Cell Death","s":"What is the role of the extrinsic pathway in embryonic nervous system development?","o":["Excess neurons are removed when a ligand binds a death receptor","Neurons are activated to divide when a ligand binds a death receptor","Neurons release cytochrome c into the blood","Neurons undergo necrosis with inflammation","Neurons convert to squamous cells"],"e":"The extrinsic (death receptor) pathway depends on death receptors, mostly in the TNF receptor family. In embryonic nervous system development more neurons are made than needed, and the excess are removed when a ligand binds a death receptor, activating caspases.","w":["Death receptors trigger death, not division.","Cytochrome c is released inside cells in the intrinsic pathway.","Developmental removal is apoptotic, not necrotic.","Metaplasia is unrelated."],"k":"Extrinsic: ligand + death receptor → apoptosis.","rn":[["","What starts the extrinsic pathway?","What starts the extrinsic pathway?","Receptor-ligand interactions; On death receptors"]]},{"id":43042,"t":"Cell Death","s":"At what point does apoptosis become impossible to stop?","o":["Once caspases are active","As soon as BH3 sensors detect injury","When Bcl-2 is expressed","When the stressor is removed","Apoptosis can always be reversed"],"e":"Apoptosis is reversible up to a point: if the stressor is removed, sensors stop and blocking proteins (Bcl-2) can halt the pathway. Once caspases are active they break down the cell's proteins and there is no stopping it.","w":["Detection can still be reversed.","Bcl-2 blocks apoptosis.","Removing the stressor can halt early stages.","It becomes irreversible after caspase activation."],"k":"Active caspases = point of no return.","rn":[["","When does apoptosis become irreversible?","When does apoptosis become irreversible?","Once caspases are active"],["","What can stop intrinsic apoptosis if the stressor is removed?","What can stop intrinsic apoptosis if the stressor is removed?","Block proteins (Bcl-2, Bcl-x); Before caspases are active"]]},{"id":43043,"t":"Cell Death","s":"In this diagram of apoptosis, where do the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways converge?","o":["At caspase activation","At the BH3 sensors","At the death receptor","At cytochrome c release","They never converge"],"e":"The left (intrinsic) pathway runs from cell injury through BH3 sensors and the Bcl-2 family to cytochrome c release; the right (extrinsic) pathway involves receptor-ligand interactions and adaptor proteins. Both converge on caspase activation, then endonuclease activation and cytoskeleton breakdown, forming an apoptotic body taken up by a phagocyte.","w":["BH3 sensors are intrinsic only.","Death receptors are extrinsic only.","Cytochrome c is intrinsic only.","They converge on caspases."],"k":"Both apoptotic pathways converge on caspases.","f":"ci14","rn":[["","Where do the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways converge?","Where do the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways converge?","Caspase activation"]]},{"id":43044,"t":"Cell Death","a":["caspases","caspase"],"s":"Name the family of specialised proteases that carry out programmed cell death.","e":"Caspases are specialised proteases that cleave other proteins to carry out apoptosis; both intrinsic and extrinsic pathways converge on caspase activation.","k":"Caspases execute apoptosis.","rn":[["","Caspases","Caspases","Specialised proteases; Cleave proteins for apoptosis"]]},{"id":43045,"t":"Cell Death","a":["cytochrome c","cytochrome-c"],"s":"Which mitochondrial protein is released into the cytoplasm in the intrinsic pathway of apoptosis?","e":"In the intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway, Bcl-2 family effectors act on mitochondria, which release cytochrome c and other pro-apoptotic proteins, leading to caspase activation.","k":"Cytochrome c release = intrinsic apoptosis.","rn":[["","What does the intrinsic pathway rely on?","What does the intrinsic pathway rely on?","Mitochondria; Cytochrome C release into cytoplasm"]]},{"id":43046,"t":"Cell Death","s":"How do phagocytes recognise dying cells, according to the speaker notes?","o":["Pattern recognition receptors detect patterns from dead or dying cells","They detect troponin and creatine kinase released into the blood by the dying cell","They respond to cytochrome c in the bloodstream","They bind antibodies coating the apoptotic body","They cannot recognise dying cells"],"e":"Phagocytes have pattern recognition receptors that identify components of dead or dying cells, given in the speaker notes as DAMPs (death associated molecular patterns). Apoptotic bodies display ligands for phagocytic cell receptors.","w":["Troponin is a blood marker of muscle necrosis.","Cytochrome c acts inside the cell.","Antibody coating is not the mechanism described.","They do recognise dying cells."],"k":"Phagocyte PRRs recognise dying-cell patterns.","rn":[["","How do phagocytes recognise dying cells?","How do phagocytes recognise dying cells?","Pattern recognition receptors; Detect DAMPs"]]},{"id":43047,"t":"Cell Death","s":"A cell is starved of nutrients during restricted blood flow. Which process lets it digest part of itself to survive?","o":["Autophagy","Necrosis","Metaplasia","Hyperplasia","Mitosis"],"e":"Autophagy is cellular self-eating in response to lack of nutrients. Autophagosomes made from ER membranes engulf part of the cytoplasm and organelles; digestion of the contents provides nutrients for survival. It can occur when blood flow is restricted and can be followed by apoptosis.","w":["Necrosis is uncontrolled death.","Metaplasia is cell-type replacement.","Hyperplasia is more cells.","Mitosis is division."],"k":"Nutrient lack → autophagy (self-eating).","rn":[["","Autophagy","Autophagy","Cellular self-eating"],["","Autophagy is a response to what?","Autophagy is a response to what?","Lack of nutrients"],["","When can autophagy take place, apart from nutrient lack?","When can autophagy take place, apart from nutrient lack?","When blood flow is restricted"]]},{"id":43048,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Put autophagy in order: (P) fusion with lysosomes; (Q) nutrient depletion signal; (R) contents used as nutrients; (S) organelles wrapped into an autophagic vacuole; (T) enzymatic digestion.","o":["Q → S → P → T → R","S → Q → P → T → R","Q → P → S → T → R","Q → S → T → P → R","R → Q → S → P → T"],"e":"Nutrient depletion sends an autophagy signal; a membrane wraps cytoplasmic organelles into an autophagic vacuole; it fuses with lysosomes; the contents are enzymatically digested; the products are used as sources of nutrients.","w":["The signal comes first.","Wrapping precedes lysosome fusion.","Digestion follows lysosome fusion.","Using nutrients is the final step."],"k":"Signal → wrap → fuse with lysosome → digest → nutrients.","rn":[["","What happens to autophagosomes after forming?","What happens to autophagosomes after forming?","Fuse with lysosomes; Contents digested into nutrients"]]},{"id":43049,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Which statement about autophagy is correct?","o":["It can be protective but may lead to apoptosis if excessive","It always causes inflammation by spilling the cell's enzymes into the tissue","It is the same as necrosis","It increases cell size","It happens only in dividing cells"],"e":"Autophagy can be protective, giving biomolecules for energy. But if the cell has eaten too much of itself, or the stressor is not removed, it can lead to apoptosis.","w":["Autophagy is not described as inflammatory.","It is distinct from necrosis.","Increased size is hypertrophy.","It is a stress response, not limited to dividing cells."],"k":"Autophagy: protective, but excess → apoptosis.","rn":[["","Is autophagy protective?","Is autophagy protective?","Can be; Or leads to apoptosis; If excessive"]]},{"id":43050,"t":"Cell Death","a":["autophagy"],"s":"What term describes cellular \"self-eating\" in response to lack of nutrients?","e":"Autophagy is cellular self-eating: autophagosomes engulf cytoplasm and organelles, whose digestion provides nutrients for survival.","k":"Autophagy = self-eating.","rn":[["","Autophagy","Autophagy","Cellular self-eating"]]},{"id":43051,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Which statement about necrosis is correct?","o":["The membrane falls apart, contents leak and inflammation follows","The membrane stays intact and forms apoptotic bodies","It is a tightly regulated physiological process in development","It causes no inflammatory response","It is executed by caspases"],"e":"Necrosis is death due to metabolic failure or disruption of the cell membrane. The membrane falls apart and the cytoplasm, including enzymes, leaks into the surrounding tissue, inducing an inflammatory response with neutrophils and macrophages recruited.","w":["Apoptotic bodies form in apoptosis.","Regulated developmental death is apoptosis.","Necrosis causes inflammation.","Caspases execute apoptosis."],"k":"Necrosis: membrane rupture, leakage, inflammation.","rn":[["","What happens to the cell membrane in necrosis?","What happens to the cell membrane in necrosis?","Falls apart; Cytoplasm and enzymes leak out"]]},{"id":43052,"t":"Cell Death","s":"How can necrosis of the heart be detected in a blood sample?","o":["A raised troponin or creatine kinase","A raised alkaline phosphatase","A low haemoglobin","A high CD4 count","A positive HIV antibody"],"e":"Necrosis can be detected from blood by measuring specific proteins: cardiac muscle necrosis gives a raised troponin or creatine kinase; liver epithelium necrosis gives a raised alkaline phosphatase (speaker notes).","w":["Alkaline phosphatase is given for liver necrosis.","Haemoglobin reflects anaemia.","CD4 relates to HIV.","HIV antibodies relate to HIV."],"k":"Cardiac necrosis: troponin, creatine kinase.","rn":[["","Which 2 proteins detect cardiac muscle necrosis?","Which 2 proteins detect cardiac muscle necrosis?","Troponin; Creatine kinase"]]},{"id":43053,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Why can necrosis spread damage beyond the cells that first died?","o":["Leaked contents trigger inflammation, which damages more tissue","Necrotic cells divide rapidly into the surrounding tissue","Apoptotic bodies spread to other organs","Necrosis always remains confined to one cell","Caspases spread between cells"],"e":"Necrosis triggers inflammation (the innate immune response), recruiting neutrophils and macrophages. Inflammation damages more tissue, so the damaged area spreads; it can stay local or become systemic, affecting blood flow, heart rate and blood pressure, and causing sepsis. Necrosis is usually more widespread than apoptosis.","w":["Necrotic cells are dead and do not divide.","Apoptotic bodies are a feature of apoptosis.","Necrosis is usually more widespread.","Caspases are intracellular apoptotic enzymes."],"k":"Necrosis → inflammation → wider damage, possibly sepsis.","rn":[["","Why is necrosis bad?","Why is necrosis bad?","Triggers inflammation; Widespread tissue damage"],["","Why does necrosis damage a wider area than the original injury?","Why does necrosis damage a wider area than the original injury?","Inflammation recruits neutrophils and macrophages; They damage more tissue"]]},{"id":43054,"t":"Cell Death","s":"In this necrosis diagram, which changes are still reversible?","o":["Swelling of ER and mitochondria, blebs and myelin figures","Breakdown of the plasma membrane with leakage","Amorphous densities in mitochondria with inflammation","Breakdown of the nucleus","None of the changes are reversible"],"e":"The figure shows a normal cell; reversible injury (swelling of the ER and mitochondria, blebs, myelin figures), which can recover; and progressive injury (breakdown of plasma membrane, organelles and nucleus, leakage, amorphous densities in mitochondria) leading to inflammation and necrosis.","w":["Membrane breakdown is progressive, irreversible injury.","Amorphous densities and inflammation mark necrosis.","Nuclear breakdown is progressive injury.","The early changes can recover."],"k":"Swelling, blebs, myelin figures = reversible.","f":"ci16","rn":[["","What are the signs of reversible injury on the way to necrosis?","What are the signs of reversible injury on the way to necrosis?","Swollen ER and mitochondria; Membrane blebs; Myelin figure"]]},{"id":43055,"t":"Cell Death","a":["necrosis"],"s":"What term describes uncontrolled cell death with membrane breakdown, leakage and inflammation?","e":"Necrosis is death from metabolic failure or membrane disruption: contents leak and inflammation follows. It is always pathological.","k":"Necrosis = messy, inflammatory death.","rn":[["","What are the 2 causes of necrosis?","What are the 2 causes of necrosis?","Metabolic failure; Disruption of the cell membrane"]]},{"id":43056,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Which statement is TRUE of necrosis but NOT of apoptosis?","o":["It is always pathological","It forms apoptotic bodies","It is tightly regulated","It has a role in development","It causes no inflammation"],"e":"Apoptosis is organised, programmed and tightly regulated, forms apoptotic bodies, causes no inflammation and has a physiological role in development. Necrosis is spontaneous (metabolic failure or membrane disruption), causes inflammation and is always pathological.","w":["Apoptotic bodies are apoptosis.","Tight regulation is apoptosis.","Developmental roles are apoptosis.","Lack of inflammation is apoptosis."],"k":"Necrosis is always pathological.","rn":[["","Which pathway causes most physiological and pathological apoptosis?","Which pathway causes most physiological and pathological apoptosis?","Intrinsic pathway"],["","Does a given injury always cause the same cell death?","Does a given injury always cause the same cell death?","No; it is a probability; e.g. infection: necrosis or apoptosis"]]},{"id":43057,"t":"Cell Death","s":"On electron microscopy, one dying cell looks condensed but relatively intact, another seems to have exploded. How are they classified?","o":["Condensed = apoptotic; exploded = necrotic","Condensed = necrotic; exploded = apoptotic","Both are apoptotic","Both are necrotic","Both are healthy dividing cells"],"e":"On electron microscopy, apoptotic cells are condensed but relatively intact (large vacuoles are a variable feature; one panel shows an engulfed dead cell inside a phagocyte), while a necrotic cell seems to have exploded.","w":["This reverses them.","The exploded cell is necrotic.","The intact condensed cell is apoptotic.","These are dying cells."],"k":"EM: apoptotic = condensed, intact; necrotic = exploded.","rn":[["","In electron micrographs, how does an apoptotic cell look compared with a necrotic cell?","In electron micrographs, how does an apoptotic cell look compared with a necrotic cell?","Apoptotic: condensed but relatively intact; Necrotic: seems exploded"],["","Image card: Which of A, B and C are apoptotic and which necrotic?","Which of A, B and C are apoptotic and which necrotic?","A, B: apoptotic; (Condensed, intact); C: necrotic (exploded)"]]},{"id":43058,"t":"Cell Death","s":"A patient's tissue is starved of oxygen, so ATP falls and energy-dependent functions fail. Which outcome is most likely?","o":["Cell injury then necrosis","Apoptosis without any injury","Hyperplasia","Metaplasia","Autophagy only, with no death"],"e":"Hypoxia or ischaemia reduces ATP so energy-dependent functions fail, leading to cell injury and then necrosis. Multiple injurious stimuli increasing ROS also lead to necrosis; misfolded proteins and DNA damage lead to apoptosis; inflammation can lead to either.","w":["Oxygen starvation typically causes necrosis.","Hyperplasia is an adaptation.","Metaplasia is an adaptation.","Autophagy can occur but the typical outcome listed is necrosis."],"k":"Hypoxia → ↓ATP → necrosis.","f":"ci19","rn":[["","Does a given injury always cause the same cell death?","Does a given injury always cause the same cell death?","No; it is a probability; e.g. infection: necrosis or apoptosis"],["","What are the 2 possible outcomes of irreversible cell injury?","What are the 2 possible outcomes of irreversible cell injury?","Apoptosis; Necrosis"],["","Why do cells swell in reversible injury?","Why do cells swell in reversible injury?","Failure of membrane ion pumps; Pumps are energy dependent; Cells take in water"]]},{"id":43059,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"A cell accumulates misfolded proteins in its ER after infection. Which death route is most likely?","o":["Apoptosis","Necrosis only","Hypertrophy","Atrophy","No death occurs"],"e":"The cell-death-after-injury table: mutations, cell stress or infections causing accumulation of misfolded proteins in the ER lead to apoptosis. Radiation causing DNA damage also leads to apoptosis. Hypoxia (↓ATP) and ROS lead to necrosis.","w":["Misfolded proteins typically lead to apoptosis.","Hypertrophy is an adaptation.","Atrophy is an adaptation.","The cell is likely to die."],"k":"Misfolded ER proteins → apoptosis.","rn":[["","Which 3 causes lead to apoptosis?","Which 3 causes lead to apoptosis?","Mutations; DNA damage; Infection/inflammation"],["","Radiation leads to which form of cell death?","Radiation leads to which form of cell death?","Apoptosis (via DNA damage)"]]},{"id":43060,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"Which statement about the injury-to-death table is consistent with the lecturer?","o":["It reflects probabilities, not fixed rules","Every cause always leads to the same death route","Infection always leads to apoptosis","Radiation always leads to necrosis","Lack of oxygen never leads to necrosis"],"e":"The lecturer stressed this is not black and white but about probability: radiation most likely leads to apoptosis; infection can lead to either necrosis or apoptosis; damage to biomolecules or lack of oxygen most likely leads to necrosis.","w":["It is probabilistic.","Infection can lead to either.","Radiation most likely leads to apoptosis.","Lack of oxygen most likely leads to necrosis."],"k":"Death route after injury is probabilistic."},{"id":43061,"t":"Cell Death","s":"A kidney infarct shows preserved tissue architecture, faint cell outlines, no nuclear staining and strong eosin staining. Which type of necrosis is this?","o":["Coagulative","Colliquative (liquefactive), with tissue that has turned to liquid","Caseous, with cheese-like structureless tissue","Fat necrosis, from leaked pancreatic lipases","Fibrinoid, from immune complexes in vessel walls"],"e":"Coagulative necrosis, the most common, preserves tissue architecture for at least several days with a firm texture, faint cell outlines, coagulated proteins, no nuclear stain and strong eosin staining. It is typical of infarcts (ischaemic necrosis) in all solid organs except the brain.","w":["Colliquative necrosis liquefies tissue (brain, abscesses).","Caseous necrosis is cheesy and structureless (TB).","Fat necrosis involves pancreatic lipases.","Fibrinoid necrosis is in vessel walls."],"k":"Coagulative: architecture kept, no nuclei, strong eosin; infarcts.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of necrosis is this? How can you tell?","What type of necrosis is this? How can you tell?","Coagulative necrosis; Faint cell outlines, no nuclei; Strong eosin stain"],["","Coagulative necrosis: 2 protein features?","Coagulative necrosis: 2 protein features?","Coagulated proteins; Strong eosin stain"]]},{"id":43062,"t":"Cell Death","s":"A patient has a stroke, and the infarcted brain tissue turns into a viscous liquid. Which type of necrosis is this?","o":["Colliquative (liquefactive)","Coagulative, which keeps the tissue architecture","Caseous, which is cheese-like","Fat necrosis","Gangrene"],"e":"Colliquative (liquefactive) necrosis is seen in the brain (hypoxic death in the CNS) and in focal bacterial (occasionally fungal) infections. Leucocyte enzymes digest the tissue into a viscous liquid; if from acute inflammation it forms creamy yellow pus. Coagulative necrosis is typical of infarcts in all solid organs except the brain.","w":["Coagulative necrosis does not occur in brain infarcts.","Caseous necrosis is typical of TB.","Fat necrosis involves adipose tissue.","Gangrene is necrosis with putrefaction."],"k":"Brain infarct → colliquative (liquefactive) necrosis.","rn":[["","Colliquative necrosis is seen in which infections?","Colliquative necrosis is seen in which infections?","Focal bacterial; Occasionally fungal"],["","Image card: What type of necrosis is this? Which tissue?","What type of necrosis is this? Which tissue?","Colliquative necrosis; Brain (liquefied)"],["","What does tissue become in colliquative necrosis?","What does tissue become in colliquative necrosis?","Viscous liquid; Removed eventually by phagocytes"]]},{"id":43063,"t":"Cell Death","s":"A lung biopsy from a patient with tuberculosis shows yellow-white, friable, cheese-like tissue with macrophages forming a granuloma at its edge. Which type of necrosis?","o":["Caseous, which is cheese-like and friable","Coagulative","Colliquative","Fibrinoid","Fat"],"e":"Caseous (cheese-like) necrosis is yellow-white and friable, common in tuberculosis. The tissue is structureless with no clear cell outlines (architecture obliterated), amorphous granular pink, with macrophages at the edge forming a granuloma.","w":["Coagulative necrosis keeps the architecture.","Colliquative necrosis liquefies tissue.","Fibrinoid necrosis is in vessel walls.","Fat necrosis involves fat cells."],"k":"TB → caseous necrosis with granuloma.","rn":[["","What does caseous necrosis look like grossly?","What does caseous necrosis look like grossly?","Cheese-like; Friable yellow-white"],["","What does caseous necrosis mean?","What does caseous necrosis mean?","Cheese-like; Friable yellow-white necrotic area"]]},{"id":43064,"t":"Cell Death","s":"A patient with acute pancreatitis has chalky areas in the peritoneal fat. Which mechanism explains this necrosis?","o":["Leaked pancreatic lipases liquefy fat-cell membranes and split triglycerides","Antigen-antibody complexes deposit in the walls of the peritoneal blood vessels","Leucocyte enzymes digest brain tissue into a liquid","Ischaemia preserves the tissue architecture with strong eosin","Putrefaction of dead tissue"],"e":"Fat necrosis occurs in pancreatic disease (acute pancreatitis): leaked pancreatic lipases liquefy fat-cell membranes and split triglycerides, in the pancreas and peritoneal cavity.","w":["That is fibrinoid necrosis.","That is colliquative necrosis.","That is coagulative necrosis.","That is gangrene."],"k":"Fat necrosis: pancreatic lipases in pancreatitis.","rn":[["","What do lipases do in fat necrosis?","What do lipases do in fat necrosis?","Split triglyceride esters; Liquefy fat cell membranes"]]},{"id":43065,"t":"Cell Death","s":"A vessel wall biopsy shows a bright pink, amorphous ring on H&amp;E. What type of necrosis is this and what causes it?","o":["Fibrinoid; antigen-antibody complexes in the vessel wall","Caseous necrosis, caused by tuberculosis infecting the vessel wall","Fat; pancreatic lipases","Colliquative; hypoxic brain injury","Coagulative; an infarct"],"e":"Fibrinoid necrosis results from antigen-antibody complexes deposited in blood vessel walls (also in severe hypertension), appearing bright pink and amorphous on H&amp;E. Antigens and antibodies stuck on the vessel wall start inflammation.","w":["Caseous necrosis is cheesy and linked to TB.","Fat necrosis involves pancreatic lipases.","Colliquative necrosis liquefies brain tissue.","Coagulative necrosis is typical of solid organ infarcts."],"k":"Fibrinoid: immune complexes in vessel walls, bright pink.","rn":[["","What causes fibrinoid necrosis?","What causes fibrinoid necrosis?","Antigen-antibody complexes in vessel walls; Severe hypertension"],["","Image card: What type of necrosis is this? What causes it?","What type of necrosis is this? What causes it?","Fibrinoid necrosis; Antigen-antibody deposition in vessel wall"]]},{"id":43066,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Which type of necrosis is defined as necrosis with putrefaction?","o":["Gangrene","Fibrinoid","Caseous","Coagulative","Fat"],"e":"Gangrene is necrosis with putrefaction (rotting). The other types are coagulative, colliquative, fat, fibrinoid and caseous.","w":["Fibrinoid is in vessel walls.","Caseous is cheese-like (TB).","Coagulative keeps architecture.","Fat involves adipose tissue."],"k":"Gangrene = necrosis + putrefaction.","rn":[["","What is meant by Gangrene?","What is meant by Gangrene?","Necrosis with putrefaction"]]},{"id":43067,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Why does coagulative necrosis stain strongly with eosin?","o":["Dead cells lose their nuclei, leaving protein remnants","The nuclei multiply and stain heavily","Fat droplets absorb the eosin","Bacteria in the dead tissue are acid-fast and take up the eosin","The tissue turns into liquid"],"e":"Coagulative necrosis stains strongly with eosin because the dead cells have lost their nuclei and leave a mass of cell remnants, mostly proteins.","w":["Nuclei are lost, not multiplied.","Fat is not the reason.","Acid-fast staining is unrelated.","Liquefaction is colliquative necrosis."],"k":"No nuclei + protein remnants → strong eosin.","rn":[["","Why does coagulative necrosis stain strongly with eosin?","Why does coagulative necrosis stain strongly with eosin?","Mass of protein remnants"]]},{"id":43068,"t":"Cell Death","s":"In this panel of necrosis examples, which type appears as a bright pink amorphous ring in a vessel wall?","o":["Fibrinoid necrosis","Coagulative necrosis","Colliquative necrosis","Caseous necrosis","Fat necrosis"],"e":"The examples slide shows coagulative (faint outlines, no nuclear stain, strong eosin), colliquative (liquefied tissue), caseous in the lung (cheesy yellow-white) and fat necrosis in the pancreas on the top row; and fibrinoid necrosis below, a bright pink amorphous ring in a vessel wall from deposited antigens and antibodies.","w":["Coagulative necrosis keeps outlines in solid tissue.","Colliquative necrosis is liquefied.","Caseous necrosis is cheesy.","Fat necrosis is in the pancreas."],"k":"Fibrinoid: pink ring in the vessel wall.","f":"ci18","rn":[["","Image card: What type of necrosis is this? What causes it?","What type of necrosis is this? What causes it?","Fibrinoid necrosis; Antigen-antibody deposition in vessel wall"],["","What causes fibrinoid necrosis?","What causes fibrinoid necrosis?","Antigen-antibody complexes in vessel walls; Severe hypertension"],["","What does fibrinoid necrosis look like histologically?","What does fibrinoid necrosis look like histologically?","Bright pink; Amorphous"]]},{"id":43069,"t":"Cell Death","a":["coagulative necrosis","coagulative"],"s":"What is the most common type of necrosis, typical of infarcts in solid organs except the brain?","e":"Coagulative necrosis is the most common type, typical of infarcts (ischaemic necrosis) in all solid organs except the brain.","k":"Coagulative = most common, solid organ infarcts.","rn":[["","Coagulative necrosis is characteristic of infarcts in which organs?","Coagulative necrosis is characteristic of infarcts in which organs?","All solid organs except brain"]]},{"id":43070,"t":"Cell Death","a":["caseous necrosis","caseous"],"s":"What type of cheese-like necrosis is common in tuberculosis?","e":"Caseous necrosis is yellow-white, friable and cheese-like, common in tuberculosis, with macrophages forming a granuloma at its edge.","k":"Caseous necrosis: TB.","rn":[["","Caseous necrosis is common in what?","Caseous necrosis is common in what?","Tuberculosis"],["","What does caseous necrosis look like grossly?","What does caseous necrosis look like grossly?","Cheese-like; Friable yellow-white"],["","What does caseous necrosis mean?","What does caseous necrosis mean?","Cheese-like; Friable yellow-white necrotic area"]]},{"id":43071,"t":"Cell Death","a":["granuloma"],"s":"What term describes the nodule of macrophages that forms at the edge of caseous necrosis?","e":"In caseous necrosis, macrophages at the edge form a granuloma.","k":"Granuloma: macrophages around caseous necrosis.","rn":[["","Caseous necrosis surrounded by macrophages forms what?","Caseous necrosis surrounded by macrophages forms what?","Granuloma"]]},{"id":43072,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"Which pairing of growth mechanism and example is correct?","o":["Accretionary: bone growth by adding extracellular tissue","Multiplicative: muscle growth by larger cells","Auxetic: embryonic development, by increasing the number of cells","Accretionary: muscle growth by more cells","Auxetic: bone growth by adding matrix"],"e":"Tissue growth mechanisms: multiplicative (increased cell numbers, e.g. embryonic development); auxetic (increased cell size, e.g. muscle tissue growth); accretionary (increased extracellular tissue, e.g. bone growth).","w":["Muscle growth is auxetic (cell size).","Embryonic development is multiplicative.","Muscle growth is auxetic, not accretionary.","Bone growth is accretionary."],"k":"Multiplicative = number; auxetic = size; accretionary = matrix.","rn":[["","What is meant by Accretionary Growth?","What is meant by Accretionary Growth?","Increase in extracellular tissue; e.g. bone tissue growth"]]},{"id":43073,"t":"Tissue Organisation","a":["auxetic"],"s":"What term describes tissue growth by increasing cell size, as in muscle?","e":"Auxetic growth is growth by increased cell size, as in muscle tissue growth. Multiplicative growth increases cell number; accretionary growth increases extracellular tissue.","k":"Auxetic = growth by cell size.","rn":[["","What is meant by Auxetic Growth?","What is meant by Auxetic Growth?","Increase in cell size; e.g. muscle tissue growth"]]},{"id":43074,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"Which are the four main tissue types?","o":["Epithelial, muscle, nervous and connective","Blood, bone, skin and fat","Labile, stable, permanent and stem","Apoptotic, necrotic, autophagic and normal","Columnar, squamous, cuboidal and glandular"],"e":"The four main tissue types are epithelial, muscle, nervous and connective.","w":["These are particular tissues, not the four main types.","These describe proliferative capacity.","These describe cell death types.","These are epithelial cell shapes."],"k":"Four tissue types: epithelial, muscle, nervous, connective.","rn":[["","What are the 4 main types of tissue?","What are the 4 main types of tissue?","Epithelial; Muscle; Nervous; Connective"]]},{"id":43075,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"Which cells are classed as labile?","o":["Surface epithelium, epidermis and haematopoietic stem cells","Hepatocytes and renal tubular cells, which divide slowly when stimulated","Neurons and cardiac muscle","Red blood cells and keratinocytes","Skeletal muscle only"],"e":"Labile cells proliferate continuously with rapid turnover and are regenerative: surface epithelial cells, skin (epidermis), epithelial cells of ducts and the GI tract, and haematopoietic stem cells. Stable: hepatocytes, renal tubular cells. Permanent: red blood cells, keratinocytes, neurons, cardiac and skeletal muscle.","w":["These are stable cells.","These are permanent cells.","These are permanent cells.","Skeletal muscle is permanent."],"k":"Labile: epithelia, epidermis, haematopoietic stem cells.","rn":[["","Labile cells: which stem cells?","Labile cells: which stem cells?","Haematopoietic stem cells"],["","Labile cells: 3 epithelial examples?","Labile cells: 3 epithelial examples?","Surface epithelium; Epidermal skin cells; GI and duct epithelium"]]},{"id":43076,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"A person donates part of their liver, and the remaining liver regrows. Which cell class explains this?","o":["Stable cells: hepatocytes can divide in response to specific stimuli","Labile cells that never stop dividing, like the epidermis","Permanent cells that cannot divide","Terminally differentiated neurons","Red blood cells from the bone marrow"],"e":"Stable cells can regenerate and proliferate but divide slowly (quiescent tissues), e.g. hepatocytes and renal tubular cells. Hepatocytes are not stem cells but can divide in response to specific stimuli, so the liver regenerates and a person can donate part of their liver.","w":["Hepatocytes are stable, not labile.","Hepatocytes can divide.","Neurons are permanent.","Red cells are permanent and not the source."],"k":"Liver regeneration: stable hepatocytes.","rn":[["","Why can hepatocytes regenerate the liver?","Why can hepatocytes regenerate the liver?","Stable cells; Divide on specific stimuli"]]},{"id":43077,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"After a myocardial infarction, why is the damaged heart muscle not replaced by new muscle cells?","o":["Cardiac muscle cells are permanent, with no regenerative capacity","Cardiac muscle cells are labile and divide too quickly to form organised muscle","Cardiac muscle cells are stable and divide slowly","Heart muscle contains a large stem cell pool","Cardiac cells convert into hepatocytes"],"e":"Permanent cells have no regenerative capacity; they are terminally differentiated and cannot proliferate: red blood cells, keratinocytes, neurons, cardiac and skeletal muscle. Neurons and myocytes are in G0 with the cell cycle turned off.","w":["Cardiac muscle is not labile.","Cardiac muscle is not stable.","Heart muscle lacks a regenerative stem pool.","Cells do not convert between tissue types this way."],"k":"Cardiac muscle is permanent: no regeneration.","rn":[["","What is meant by Permanent Cells?","What is meant by Permanent Cells?","No regenerative capacity; Terminally differentiated, cannot proliferate"],["","Permanent cells: 2 more examples?","Permanent cells: 2 more examples?","Neurons; Cardiac, skeletal muscle"]]},{"id":43078,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"Which pairing of cell type and class is NOT correct?","o":["Neurons: labile","Epidermis: labile","Hepatocytes: stable","Renal tubular cells: stable","Cardiac muscle: permanent"],"e":"Neurons are permanent cells: terminally differentiated with no regenerative capacity (although some areas of CNS regeneration have been identified and this is still being worked out). The other pairings are correct.","w":["The epidermis is labile.","Hepatocytes are stable.","Renal tubular cells are stable.","Cardiac muscle is permanent."],"k":"Neurons are permanent.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of cells by regenerative ability?","What are the 3 types of cells by regenerative ability?","Labile; Stable; Permanent"],["","Permanent cells: 2 more examples?","Permanent cells: 2 more examples?","Neurons; Cardiac, skeletal muscle"]]},{"id":43079,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"Red blood cells are listed as permanent. How are new red cells made?","o":["From haematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow","By existing red cells dividing in the circulating blood","By hepatocytes dividing","By cardiac muscle differentiating","They are never replaced"],"e":"Red blood cells do not regenerate themselves: they and all immune cells are terminal. New blood cells come from haematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow, which are labile.","w":["Red cells do not divide.","Hepatocytes make liver, not red cells.","Cardiac muscle does not make blood cells.","They are replaced from marrow stem cells."],"k":"New red cells come from marrow stem cells.","rn":[["","Why are red cells terminal yet blood regenerates?","Why are red cells terminal yet blood regenerates?","From haematopoietic stem cells; In bone marrow"]]},{"id":43080,"t":"Tissue Organisation","a":["labile","labile cells"],"s":"What term describes cells that proliferate continuously with rapid turnover, such as gut epithelium?","e":"Labile cells proliferate continuously with rapid turnover and are regenerative, e.g. surface epithelium, epidermis, GI epithelium and haematopoietic stem cells.","k":"Labile = continuous division.","rn":[["","What is meant by Labile Cells?","What is meant by Labile Cells?","Proliferate continuously; Rapid cell turnover"]]},{"id":43081,"t":"Tissue Organisation","a":["stable","stable cells"],"s":"What term describes cells that can regenerate but divide slowly, such as hepatocytes?","e":"Stable cells can regenerate and proliferate but divide slowly (quiescent tissues), e.g. hepatocytes and renal tubular cells.","k":"Stable = slow, inducible division.","rn":[["","What is meant by Stable Cells?","What is meant by Stable Cells?","Can proliferate but divide slowly; Slow turnover"]]},{"id":43082,"t":"Tissue Organisation","a":["permanent","permanent cells"],"s":"What term describes terminally differentiated cells with no regenerative capacity, such as neurons?","e":"Permanent cells have no regenerative capacity: terminally differentiated and unable to proliferate, e.g. red cells, keratinocytes, neurons, cardiac and skeletal muscle.","k":"Permanent = no division.","rn":[["","What is meant by Permanent Cells?","What is meant by Permanent Cells?","No regenerative capacity; Terminally differentiated, cannot proliferate"],["","Permanent cells: 2 more examples?","Permanent cells: 2 more examples?","Neurons; Cardiac, skeletal muscle"]]},{"id":43083,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"Which statement about proliferative tissues is consistent with the lecture?","o":["Fibroblasts and lymphocytes periodically enter and exit the cell cycle","All cells in the body are actively dividing through the cell cycle at all times","Liver cells can never re-enter the cell cycle","Neurons divide continuously throughout adult life","Skin has no stem cell reservoir"],"e":"Many human cells divide a finite number of times; most cells are in G0, reversibly for some. Neurons and myocytes are terminally differentiated. Most liver cells are in G0 but can restart the cycle after damage. Fibroblasts and lymphocytes periodically enter and exit the cycle. Epithelium, skin and blood contain regularly cycling stem/progenitor reservoirs.","w":["Most cells are in G0.","Liver cells can restart the cycle after damage.","Neurons are terminally differentiated.","Skin relies on stem cell reservoirs."],"k":"Fibroblasts and lymphocytes cycle intermittently.","rn":[["","Which 2 cell types periodically enter and exit the cell cycle?","Which 2 cell types periodically enter and exit the cell cycle?","Fibroblasts; Lymphocytes"]]},{"id":43084,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"Where are the stem cell pools of labile tissues found, according to the lecturer?","o":["GI epithelial crypts and the basal layer of the skin","The cardiac muscle wall of the left ventricle","Mature red blood cells","Terminal neurons of the brain","Hepatocytes of the liver"],"e":"Labile tissues contain pools of stem cells, e.g. in the GI epithelium (crypts) and the basal cell layer of the skin. Hepatocytes are stable cells that are not stem cells but can divide when stimulated.","w":["Cardiac muscle is permanent.","Red cells are permanent.","Neurons are permanent.","Hepatocytes are stable, not stem cells."],"k":"Stem pools: gut crypts, basal skin layer.","rn":[["","Where are adult stem cell pools found in skin and gut?","Where are adult stem cell pools found in skin and gut?","Skin basal layer; GI tract crypts"],["","Where are stem cells found in skin and gut?","Where are stem cells found in skin and gut?","Basal cell layer of skin; Crypts of the GI tract"]]},{"id":43085,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"Which statement about labile/stable/permanent versus stem/progenitor/differentiated terms is correct?","o":["They overlap but are not identical: physiological vs biomedical terms","They mean exactly the same thing and can be swapped freely","Labile always means stem cell","Permanent always means progenitor","Stable cells are always stem cells"],"e":"The lecturer noted that labile/stable/permanent are physiological terms, while stem/progenitor/differentiated are biomedical terms; they overlap but are not identical. For example, hepatocytes are stable but not stem cells.","w":["They are not identical.","Labile tissues contain stem cells, but labile ≠ stem cell.","Permanent cells are terminally differentiated.","Hepatocytes are stable but not stem cells."],"k":"Physiological vs biomedical terms overlap but differ.","rn":[["","Are stem, progenitor and differentiated cell terms physiological or biomedical?","Are stem, progenitor and differentiated cell terms physiological or biomedical?","Biomedical terms"]]},{"id":43086,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"Which cells did the lecturer describe as very robust, surviving almost anything in culture?","o":["Fibroblasts","Luminal epithelial cells","Neurons","Red blood cells","Cardiomyocytes"],"e":"Some cells are more prone to damage: luminal epithelial cells are fragile, while fibroblasts are very robust and survive almost anything in culture.","w":["Luminal epithelial cells are fragile.","Neurons are not described as robust in culture.","Red cells are not described this way.","Cardiomyocytes are not described this way."],"k":"Fibroblasts are robust; luminal epithelium is fragile.","rn":[["","Fibroblasts vs epithelial cells under harsh treatment?","Fibroblasts vs epithelial cells under harsh treatment?","Fibroblasts: robust, survive; Epithelial: more prone to damage"],["","Why are epithelial cells more prone to damage than fibroblasts?","Why are epithelial cells more prone to damage than fibroblasts?","Epithelial cells; Less robust and more complex"]]},{"id":43087,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"A patient has two injuries: a skin graze and a small brain infarct. Which outcome is most likely?","o":["The skin regenerates; the brain cannot replace its neurons","Both regenerate completely within days from their resident stem cell pools","Neither tissue can regenerate at all","The brain regenerates faster than the skin","Both form coagulative necrosis"],"e":"Recovery depends on the type of injury and the proliferative and differentiation capacity of the injured cells. The epidermis is labile with basal stem cells and regenerates; neurons are permanent cells. Brain infarcts show colliquative, not coagulative, necrosis.","w":["Neurons do not regenerate in the same way.","Skin is labile and regenerates.","Skin regenerates faster.","Brain infarcts are colliquative."],"k":"Labile skin regenerates; permanent neurons do not."},{"id":43088,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Which pairing of necrosis type and typical setting is correct?","o":["Colliquative: brain and focal bacterial infections","Coagulative: infarcts in the brain after a stroke","Caseous: acute pancreatitis","Fat: tuberculosis","Fibrinoid: liver infarcts only"],"e":"Coagulative: infarcts in solid organs except the brain. Colliquative: brain (hypoxic CNS death) and focal bacterial infections. Caseous: tuberculosis. Fat: acute pancreatitis. Fibrinoid: immune complexes in vessel walls (and severe hypertension).","w":["Brain infarcts are colliquative.","Pancreatitis causes fat necrosis.","TB causes caseous necrosis.","Fibrinoid necrosis is in vessel walls."],"k":"Match necrosis type to setting.","rn":[["","Colliquative necrosis is seen in which infections?","Colliquative necrosis is seen in which infections?","Focal bacterial; Occasionally fungal"]]},{"id":43089,"t":"Cell Death","s":"A focal bacterial infection forms an abscess containing creamy yellow pus. Which type of necrosis is this?","o":["Colliquative","Coagulative","Caseous","Fat","Fibrinoid"],"e":"Colliquative necrosis occurs in the brain and in focal bacterial (occasionally fungal) infections. Leucocyte enzymes digest the tissue into a viscous liquid; if from acute inflammation, it forms creamy yellow pus.","w":["Coagulative keeps the architecture.","Caseous is cheesy, typical of TB.","Fat necrosis involves lipases.","Fibrinoid is in vessel walls."],"k":"Abscess pus = colliquative necrosis.","rn":[["","Colliquative necrosis is seen in which infections?","Colliquative necrosis is seen in which infections?","Focal bacterial; Occasionally fungal"]]},{"id":43090,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Which statement about how dead tissue is cleared in coagulative necrosis is correct?","o":["Leucocytes are recruited and debris is removed by neutrophils and macrophages","The dead cells divide and replace themselves within a few days","Dead tissue is cleared by apoptotic bodies without inflammation","Dead tissue is never cleared","Dead tissue converts into fat"],"e":"In coagulative necrosis, leucocytes are recruited, lysosomal enzymes digest the dead cells, and debris is removed by neutrophils and macrophages. The dead area stays visible until it is cleared.","w":["Dead cells do not divide.","Necrosis involves inflammation.","Debris is cleared.","Dead tissue does not become fat."],"k":"Necrotic debris is cleared by neutrophils and macrophages.","rn":[["","How are dead cells removed in coagulative necrosis?","How are dead cells removed in coagulative necrosis?","Lysosomal enzymes digest; Neutrophils, macrophages phagocytose"],["","Why does necrosis damage a wider area than the original injury?","Why does necrosis damage a wider area than the original injury?","Inflammation recruits neutrophils and macrophages; They damage more tissue"]]},{"id":43091,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"Which pairing of adaptation and definition is correct?","o":["Hyperplasia: increase in cell number","Hypertrophy: increase in the number of cells in the tissue","Atrophy: replacement of one adult cell type by another","Metaplasia: reduction in the size of the cells","Hyperplasia: reduction in the number of cells"],"e":"The four adaptations: hypertrophy (increase in cell size), hyperplasia (increase in cell number), atrophy (reduction in cell size) and metaplasia (replacement of one adult cell type with another adult cell type).","w":["Hypertrophy is increased cell size.","Replacement of cell type is metaplasia.","Reduced cell size is atrophy.","Hyperplasia is an increase."],"k":"Hypertrophy size↑; hyperplasia number↑; atrophy size↓; metaplasia type change.","rn":[["","Hyperplasia","Hyperplasia","Increase in cell number"]]},{"id":43092,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"What distinguishes physiologic from pathologic adaptation?","o":["Physiologic responds to normal signals; pathologic is a stress response that sacrifices function","Physiologic adaptation is always harmful, whereas pathologic adaptation is a normal response to hormones","Pathologic adaptation happens only during pregnancy","Physiologic adaptation always leads to cell death","There is no difference between them"],"e":"Physiologic adaptation is a response to hormones or signalling, or mechanical pressure (e.g. uterus enlargement in pregnancy, bones or muscles under load). Pathologic adaptation is a response to stress: cells modify their function to escape injury, at the expense of normal function.","w":["Physiologic adaptation is normal.","Pregnancy is a physiologic example.","Adaptation is an alternative to death.","They differ in cause and cost."],"k":"Physiologic = normal signals; pathologic = stress, at a cost.","rn":[["","Pathologic adaptation: trade-off?","Pathologic adaptation: trade-off?","Escape injury; At cost of normal function"]]},{"id":43093,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"A weightlifter's muscles grow larger over months of training. Which adaptation explains this, and why?","o":["Hypertrophy, because skeletal muscle cells do not divide","Hyperplasia, because skeletal muscle cells divide rapidly","Metaplasia, because muscle is replaced by bone","Atrophy, because cells shrink with training","Necrosis, because exercise kills muscle cells"],"e":"Exercise-induced muscle growth is due only to skeletal muscle hypertrophy: skeletal muscle cells are multinucleated and do not divide, so muscles grow by cells getting bigger. Growing cells need more protein, which is why weightlifters are told to eat more protein.","w":["Skeletal muscle cells do not divide.","Muscle-to-bone change is pathologic metaplasia after injury.","Training enlarges cells.","Training does not cause necrosis here."],"k":"Exercised skeletal muscle: hypertrophy only.","rn":[["","Muscle growth with exercise is due to what?","Muscle growth with exercise is due to what?","Only skeletal muscle hypertrophy"],["","Why does muscle grow by hypertrophy?","Why does muscle grow by hypertrophy?","Muscle cells don't divide; Have multiple nuclei"]]},{"id":43094,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"During pregnancy the uterus enlarges greatly. Which adaptations contribute?","o":["Smooth muscle hypertrophy and hyperplasia, driven by oestrogen","Atrophy of the smooth muscle followed by metaplasia into a tougher squamous lining","Necrosis of the uterine wall","Only hyperplasia of fat cells","Only autophagy"],"e":"In pregnancy the uterus grows because smooth muscle cells enlarge (hypertrophy) in response to oestrogen; they also proliferate (hyperplasia). This is physiologic adaptation.","w":["Atrophy and metaplasia are not described here.","It is adaptation, not necrosis.","Smooth muscle, not fat, is described.","Autophagy is not the mechanism."],"k":"Pregnant uterus: smooth muscle hypertrophy + hyperplasia.","rn":[["","Image card: Normal vs pregnant uterus: what adaptation do the smooth muscle cells show? What drives…","Normal vs pregnant uterus: what adaptation do the smooth muscle cells show? What drives it?","Hypertrophy (plus hyperplasia); Oestrogen"],["","What 2 adaptations cause uterus growth in pregnancy?","What 2 adaptations cause uterus growth in pregnancy?","Smooth muscle hypertrophy; Smooth muscle hyperplasia"]]},{"id":43095,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"A patient with long-standing high blood pressure has a thick-walled left ventricle. Which adaptation has occurred?","o":["Pathologic hypertrophy of cardiomyocytes","Hyperplasia of cardiomyocytes, which divide to thicken the wall","Physiologic atrophy","Squamous metaplasia","Caseous necrosis"],"e":"With increased blood pressure, cardiomyocytes become hypertrophic, enlarging the heart: pathologic hypertrophy (adaptation to increased load). Cardiomyocytes are permanent cells and do not divide, so the response is hypertrophy, not hyperplasia.","w":["Cardiomyocytes do not proliferate.","The heart wall thickens, not shrinks.","Metaplasia is cell-type replacement.","Caseous necrosis is typical of TB."],"k":"Hypertension → cardiomyocyte hypertrophy.","rn":[["","How do cardiomyocytes respond to increased blood pressure?","How do cardiomyocytes respond to increased blood pressure?","Hypertrophy; Enlargement of the heart"]]},{"id":43096,"t":"Cell Death","s":"In this heart diagram, what does the right-hand path show?","o":["Ischaemia causing reversible injury, then cell death (infarct)","Adaptation to increased load, producing hypertrophic myocytes and a thick-walled heart","A normal myocyte and normal heart","Hyperplasia of cardiomyocytes","Metaplasia of heart muscle into bone"],"e":"The centre shows a normal myocyte and heart. The left path shows adaptation to increased load: an adapted (hypertrophic) myocyte and a thick-walled heart. The right path shows ischaemia causing a reversibly injured myocyte, then cell death, seen in the heart slice as an infarct (coagulative necrosis).","w":["That is the left path.","That is the centre.","Cardiomyocytes do not proliferate.","Metaplasia is not shown."],"k":"Ischaemia → reversible injury → infarct.","f":"ci27","rn":[["","Infarct","Infarct","Area of necrosis; From ischaemia"],["","What happens to cardiomyocytes in ischaemia?","What happens to cardiomyocytes in ischaemia?","Metabolic stress; Reversible injury; Can progress to coagulative necrosis"],["","What happens to a cell after reversible injury?","What happens to a cell after reversible injury?","Survival"]]},{"id":43097,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"A patient's leg is immobilised in a cast for 8 weeks and the muscles shrink. Which mechanisms underlie this atrophy?","o":["Reduced protein synthesis, increased protein degradation and autophagy","Increased protein synthesis and cell division","Replacement of muscle by squamous epithelium","Caspase-induced apoptosis of every muscle cell, followed by replacement with scar tissue","Accretion of extracellular matrix"],"e":"Atrophy is a reduction in cell size, causing reduced tissue/organ size; cells remain functional though function may be reduced. Causes include reduced blood flow, loss of innervation, reduced workload, reduced nutrients, loss of endocrine stimulation and ageing. Mechanisms: reduced protein synthesis, increased protein degradation and autophagy.","w":["That would enlarge cells.","That would be metaplasia.","Atrophic cells remain alive and functional.","That is accretionary growth."],"k":"Atrophy: ↓synthesis, ↑degradation, autophagy.","rn":[["","What are the 3 mechanisms of atrophy?","What are the 3 mechanisms of atrophy?","Reduced protein synthesis; Increased protein degradation; Autophagy"]]},{"id":43098,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"Which of the following is NOT a listed cause of atrophy?","o":["Increased hormonal stimulation","Reduced blood flow to the tissue","Loss of innervation","Reduced workload","Ageing"],"e":"Atrophy causes: reduced blood flow, loss of innervation, reduced workload, reduced nutrient supply, loss of endocrine (hormone) stimulation and ageing. Increased hormonal stimulation drives hyperplasia or hypertrophy, not atrophy.","w":["Reduced blood flow is listed.","Loss of innervation is listed.","Reduced workload is listed.","Ageing is listed."],"k":"Atrophy follows reduced supply or stimulation."},{"id":43099,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"An older adult's brain shows reduced gyri and widened sulci. Which statement is correct?","o":["This is atrophy, which can be a normal part of ageing","This is hyperplasia of neurons","This is metaplasia of brain tissue","This is caseous necrosis","This is hypertrophy of glial cells compressing the surrounding neurons"],"e":"The atrophy slide compares a young adult brain with an ageing brain showing reduced gyri (ridges) and widened sulci (grooves). Atrophy can be a normal part of ageing: we lose neurons and tissues shrink.","w":["Neurons do not proliferate.","Metaplasia is cell-type replacement.","Caseous necrosis is typical of TB.","Brain shrinkage is not hypertrophy."],"k":"Ageing brain shrinkage = atrophy.","rn":[["","Image card: What are the 3 labelled parts of this epithelium?","What are the 3 labelled parts of this epithelium?","Basement membrane; Normal columnar epithelium; Squamous metaplasia"]]},{"id":43100,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"What is the difference between atrophy and hypotrophy, according to the lecturer?","o":["Atrophy is shrinking of cells; hypotrophy is a developmental reduction in size","They mean exactly the same thing","Hypotrophy is increase in cell size","Atrophy is increase in cell number","Hypotrophy is cell death by necrosis, whereas atrophy is programmed death by apoptosis"],"e":"Atrophy is shrinking of cells, for example from lack of nutrients. Hypotrophy is a developmental reduction in size from impaired development.","w":["They are different.","Increased size is hypertrophy.","Increased number is hyperplasia.","Hypotrophy is not necrosis."],"k":"Atrophy = shrink; hypotrophy = underdeveloped.","rn":[["","Atrophy vs hypotrophy","Atrophy vs hypotrophy","Atrophy: cells shrink; Hypotrophy: impaired development"],["","Atrophy","Atrophy","Reduction in cell size"],["","Atrophy vs hypotrophy: cause","Atrophy vs hypotrophy: cause","Atrophy: shrinking; Hypotrophy: impaired development"]]},{"id":43101,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"After part of a patient's liver is surgically removed, the remaining liver regrows. Which adaptation is this?","o":["Physiologic hyperplasia","Pathologic metaplasia","Atrophy","Hypotrophy","Coagulative necrosis of the remnant"],"e":"Hyperplasia is an increase in cell number by proliferation of differentiated or progenitor cells. Physiologic hyperplasia occurs in response to hormones or after removal of part of a tissue/organ, e.g. after part of the liver is removed the remaining cells proliferate more.","w":["Metaplasia changes cell type.","Atrophy shrinks cells.","Hypotrophy is developmental under-growth.","Necrosis is cell death."],"k":"Liver regrowth after resection = physiologic hyperplasia.","rn":[["","Physiologic hyperplasia: 2 other examples?","Physiologic hyperplasia: 2 other examples?","Liver after part removed; Breast for breastfeeding"],["","What triggers physiologic hyperplasia?","What triggers physiologic hyperplasia?","Hormonal stimuli; Removal of part of organ"]]},{"id":43102,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"A woman breastfeeds after pregnancy. Which change in her breast tissue occurred under hormonal stimulation?","o":["Hyperplasia of glandular units driven by oestrogen, progesterone and prolactin","Atrophy of breast lobules","Squamous metaplasia of the ducts in response to the hormonal changes of late pregnancy","Fat necrosis","Hypotrophy of the gland"],"e":"The mammary gland figure shows lobules before and in pregnancy, driven by oestrogen, progesterone and prolactin, with far more glandular units in the pregnant (hyperplastic) breast. Breast tissue proliferates to support breastfeeding.","w":["Lobules increase, not shrink.","Metaplasia is not described here.","Fat necrosis is from pancreatic lipases.","Hypotrophy is developmental."],"k":"Pregnant breast: hormonal hyperplasia.","rn":[["","Which 3 hormones drive mammary hyperplasia?","Which 3 hormones drive mammary hyperplasia?","Oestrogen (E); Progesterone (P); Prolactin"]]},{"id":43103,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"Why is hyperplasia associated with a risk of malignancy?","o":["Cells are dividing a lot, so loss of control can lead to cancer","Cells stop dividing completely","Cells shrink and die","It always involves necrosis","It replaces one cell type with another"],"e":"Hyperplasia is tightly controlled, but because cells are dividing a lot there is a risk of malignancy (cancer) if control fails. Neoplasia is covered in a later lecture.","w":["Hyperplasia involves increased division.","Shrinking is atrophy.","Hyperplasia is not necrosis.","Cell-type replacement is metaplasia."],"k":"Hyperplasia: risk of malignancy if control fails.","rn":[["","Most cells in the body are in which phase?","Most cells in the body are in which phase?","G0 (non-dividing)"]]},{"id":43104,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"After significant bleeding, the bone marrow produces more red cells. Which adaptation is this?","o":["Physiologic hyperplasia","Atrophy","Metaplasia","Hypertrophy of existing marrow cells only","Necrosis"],"e":"Physiologic examples of hyperplasia include bone marrow hyperplasia after bleeding (more red cells) and after infection (more white cells), breast tissue proliferation for breastfeeding, and the liver after damage.","w":["Atrophy is shrinkage.","Metaplasia changes cell type.","More cells is hyperplasia, not size alone.","Necrosis is death."],"k":"Marrow after bleeding = hyperplasia.","rn":[["","Physiologic hyperplasia: 2 bone marrow examples?","Physiologic hyperplasia: 2 bone marrow examples?","After bleeding; After infection"],["","Why bone marrow hyperplasia after bleeding/infection?","Why bone marrow hyperplasia after bleeding/infection?","Bleeding: more red cells; Infection: more white cells"]]},{"id":43105,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"A long-term smoker's bronchial lining changes from columnar to squamous epithelium. What is the main consequence?","o":["The squamous lining resists smoke but cannot secrete mucus or clear particles, and cancer risk rises","The squamous lining is more fragile, so smoke damage increases while mucus secretion and clearance stay normal","The airway gains extra cilia","The change protects completely with no downside","The epithelium turns into muscle"],"e":"In smokers' airways, fragile columnar epithelium is replaced by more robust squamous epithelium, which withstands noxious smoke chemicals, but the new epithelium cannot secrete mucus or clear small particles. Metaplasia also increases the risk of malignancy.","w":["Squamous epithelium is more robust.","The squamous lining lacks the clearance function.","There are downsides: lost function and cancer risk.","Epithelium does not become muscle."],"k":"Smokers: squamous metaplasia — tougher but loses mucus/clearance, ↑cancer risk.","rn":[["","What 2 functions does the new squamous airway epithelium lose?","What 2 functions does the new squamous airway epithelium lose?","Mucus secretion; Clearing small particles"],["","Alcohol: oesophageal metaplasia?","Alcohol: oesophageal metaplasia?","More squamous; To withstand alcohol; Cancer risk rises"]]},{"id":43106,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"How does metaplasia arise at the cellular level?","o":["Stem cells are reprogrammed to make a different cell type","Mature columnar cells directly turn into squamous cells without involving stem cells","Cells fuse to form a new tissue","Neurons migrate into the epithelium","Necrotic cells are replaced by fat"],"e":"Metaplasia is thought to arise from reprogramming of stem cell differentiation, not a change in already differentiated cells. A stem cell under a columnar epithelium is reprogrammed by the stressor to make squamous cells, which expand while the columnar cells shrink. Reprogramming changes which genes are expressed.","w":["Mature cells do not directly convert.","Cell fusion is not the mechanism.","Neurons do not migrate into epithelium.","Fat replacement is not metaplasia."],"k":"Metaplasia: reprogrammed stem cells, not converted mature cells.","rn":[["","Do differentiated cells change type in metaplasia?","Do differentiated cells change type in metaplasia?","No; Stem cells reprogrammed"],["","How is metaplasia believed to arise?","How is metaplasia believed to arise?","Reprogramming of stem cell differentiation"]]},{"id":43107,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"A patient with chronic acid reflux develops a new epithelium in the lower oesophagus. What change has occurred and why?","o":["Squamous to gastric columnar, which has more mucus to combat acid","Columnar to squamous, to resist smoke","Squamous to bone, after injury","Columnar to squamous, as the acidity makes a tougher layered lining necessary","No change; reflux causes atrophy only"],"e":"In the lower oesophagus, chronic acid reflux causes squamous to gastric columnar metaplasia; the answer to the slide's question is that columnar epithelium has more mucus to combat the acidity. Other examples: cervix (columnar to squamous due to vaginal acidity) and soft tissue (bone formation after injury).","w":["That is the smokers' airway change.","That is pathologic metaplasia in soft tissue.","That is the cervix example.","Reflux causes metaplasia."],"k":"Reflux: oesophageal squamous → columnar metaplasia.","f":"ci31","rn":[["","Why does squamous to columnar metaplasia occur in acid reflux?","Why does squamous to columnar metaplasia occur in acid reflux?","Columnar epithelium has more mucus; Combats acidity"],["","Why does columnar epithelium replace squamous in acid reflux?","Why does columnar epithelium replace squamous in acid reflux?","More mucus; Combats acidity"],["","Chronic acid reflux: which metaplasia?","Chronic acid reflux: which metaplasia?","Lower oesophagus: squamous; To gastric columnar"]]},{"id":43108,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"Which pairing of metaplasia site, change and cause is correct?","o":["Cervix: columnar to squamous; vaginal acidity","Airway: squamous to columnar; smoking","Oesophagus: columnar to squamous; acid reflux","Muscle: muscle to fat; exercise","Cervix: squamous to bone; injury"],"e":"Metaplasia examples: smokers' airways (columnar to squamous); lower oesophagus (squamous to gastric columnar, chronic acid reflux); cervix (columnar to squamous, acidity of the vaginal environment); soft tissue/muscle (bone formation, after injury).","w":["Smoking causes columnar to squamous change.","Reflux causes squamous to columnar change.","Muscle-to-fat is not a listed example.","Bone formation is in injured soft tissue."],"k":"Cervix: columnar → squamous (acidity).","rn":[["","Why does squamous to columnar metaplasia occur in acid reflux?","Why does squamous to columnar metaplasia occur in acid reflux?","Columnar epithelium has more mucus; Combats acidity"],["","Why does columnar epithelium replace squamous in acid reflux?","Why does columnar epithelium replace squamous in acid reflux?","More mucus; Combats acidity"],["","Image card: Chronic acid reflux, lower oesophagus: what change is this?","Chronic acid reflux, lower oesophagus: what change is this?","Metaplasia; Squamous to gastric columnar"]]},{"id":43109,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"Why are squamous cells more robust than a single layer of columnar cells?","o":["They are smaller, flatter and can stack in layers","They are larger and secrete more mucus to trap pollutants","They divide faster than stem cells","They contain no nucleus","They are permanent cells"],"e":"The lecturer explained that squamous cells are smaller, flatter and can stack in layers, so they are more robust; a single layer of columnar cells is more susceptible to pollutants.","w":["Columnar cells secrete mucus; squamous cells do not here.","Speed of division is not the reason.","Squamous cells have nuclei.","Classification as permanent is not the reason."],"k":"Layered squamous epithelium is tougher.","rn":[["","Why are squamous cells more robust?","Why are squamous cells more robust?","Smaller and flatter; Layer upon layer"]]},{"id":43110,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"The lecturer compared metaplasia to medical students switching to law school when there are no medical jobs. What is the key point?","o":["The same stem cells take a new differentiation path","Mature cells lose their nucleus","New cells come from outside the tissue","The original tissue dies and is replaced by scar tissue from fibroblasts","The cells stop dividing permanently"],"e":"The analogy: same people, new path. In metaplasia the same stem/progenitor cells are reprogrammed to produce a different adult cell type, by changing which genes are expressed (all cells have the same DNA).","w":["Loss of nuclei indicates death.","The new cells come from resident stem cells.","Metaplasia is not scarring.","Stem cells keep dividing."],"k":"Metaplasia: same stem cells, new path.","rn":[["","How is metaplasia believed to arise?","How is metaplasia believed to arise?","Reprogramming of stem cell differentiation"],["","Cell differentiation","Cell differentiation","Stem/progenitor cells; Become more specific type"]]},{"id":43111,"t":"Cell Adaptation","a":["hypertrophy"],"s":"What adaptation describes an increase in cell size?","e":"Hypertrophy is an increase in cell size, e.g. exercised skeletal muscle or the heart in high blood pressure.","k":"Hypertrophy = bigger cells.","rn":[["","Hypertrophy","Hypertrophy","Increase in cell size"]]},{"id":43112,"t":"Cell Adaptation","a":["hyperplasia"],"s":"What adaptation describes an increase in cell number?","e":"Hyperplasia is an increase in cell number, e.g. liver regrowth after resection, the breast in pregnancy, or bone marrow after bleeding.","k":"Hyperplasia = more cells.","rn":[["","Hyperplasia","Hyperplasia","Increase in cell number"]]},{"id":43113,"t":"Cell Adaptation","a":["atrophy"],"s":"What adaptation describes a reduction in cell size, causing reduced organ size?","e":"Atrophy is a reduction in cell size causing reduced tissue or organ size, e.g. the ageing brain or immobilised muscle.","k":"Atrophy = smaller cells.","rn":[["","Atrophy","Atrophy","Reduction in cell size"],["","What does atrophy do to tissues and organs?","What does atrophy do to tissues and organs?","Reduces their size"]]},{"id":43114,"t":"Cell Adaptation","a":["metaplasia"],"s":"What adaptation describes the replacement of one adult cell type with another adult cell type?","e":"Metaplasia is the replacement of one adult cell type with another, e.g. squamous metaplasia of smokers' airways or columnar metaplasia of the oesophagus in reflux.","k":"Metaplasia = cell type replacement.","rn":[["","Metaplasia","Metaplasia","One adult cell type; Replaced by another; Caused by cell stress"]]},{"id":43115,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"A patient's ischaemic heart muscle first shows reversible injury, then coagulative necrosis. Which statement correctly links these lecture concepts?","o":["Reduced ATP from ischaemia injures cells; if not relieved, necrosis follows","Ischaemia causes hyperplasia of cardiomyocytes","Ischaemia always causes apoptosis without any preceding injury, so no inflammation results","Coagulative necrosis is reversible","Ischaemic heart muscle liquefies like the brain"],"e":"Ischaemia reduces ATP so energy-dependent functions fail, causing cell injury and then necrosis. In the heart, metabolic stress from obstruction is a reversible injury that can progress to coagulative necrosis (an infarct). Coagulative necrosis occurs in solid organs except the brain.","w":["Cardiomyocytes do not proliferate.","Ischaemia typically leads to necrosis.","Necrosis is irreversible.","Liquefaction is typical of the brain only."],"k":"Ischaemia → ↓ATP → reversible injury → coagulative necrosis.","rn":[["","What happens to cardiomyocytes in ischaemia?","What happens to cardiomyocytes in ischaemia?","Metabolic stress; Reversible injury; Can progress to coagulative necrosis"],["","Infarct","Infarct","Area of necrosis; From ischaemia"]]},{"id":43116,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"Which adaptation would most likely occur in an organ that loses its nerve supply?","o":["Atrophy","Hyperplasia","Hypertrophy","Metaplasia","Physiologic hyperplasia"],"e":"Loss of innervation (nerve supply) is a listed cause of atrophy, along with reduced blood flow, reduced workload, reduced nutrients, loss of endocrine stimulation and ageing.","w":["Hyperplasia follows increased stimulation.","Hypertrophy follows increased load.","Metaplasia follows chronic stress such as smoke or acid.","Loss of nerve supply does not cause hyperplasia."],"k":"Denervation → atrophy.","rn":[["","Atrophy: 3 supply causes?","Atrophy: 3 supply causes?","Reduced blood flow; Reduced nutrients; Loss of endocrine stimulation"]]},{"id":43117,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Which statement summarises the possible responses of a stressed cell?","o":["It may recover, adapt or die","It can only die","It can only divide","It always becomes a stem cell","It always undergoes metaplasia"],"e":"The summary: cells may recover, adapt or die. The outcome depends on the type and duration of the external stressor and on the proliferative and differentiation capacity of the injured cells. Death can be organised (apoptosis) or spontaneous (necrosis), leading to inflammation.","w":["Recovery and adaptation are possible.","Division is not the only response.","Differentiated cells do not revert.","Metaplasia is one adaptation among several."],"k":"Stressed cells: recover, adapt or die."},{"id":43118,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"Which adaptations carry an increased risk of malignancy, according to the lecture?","o":["Hyperplasia and metaplasia","Atrophy and hypotrophy","Hypertrophy of skeletal muscle only","Autophagy and apoptosis","None of the adaptations"],"e":"Hyperplasia carries a risk of malignancy if control fails, and metaplasia increases the risk of malignancy; any reprogramming of cell behaviour carries a risk.","w":["Atrophy and hypotrophy are not described as malignancy risks.","Exercise hypertrophy is not described as a cancer risk.","Autophagy and apoptosis are not adaptations here.","Two adaptations carry risk."],"k":"Hyperplasia and metaplasia raise cancer risk.","rn":[["","What is the main risk of hyperplasia?","What is the main risk of hyperplasia?","Malignancy (cancer)"],["","What is the main risk of metaplasia?","What is the main risk of metaplasia?","Malignancy (cancer)"],["","What are the 4 adaptations?","What are the 4 adaptations?","Hypertrophy; Hyperplasia; Atrophy; Metaplasia"]]},{"id":43119,"t":"Cell Adaptation","s":"A patient's soft tissue forms bone after an injury. Which term applies?","o":["Pathologic metaplasia","Physiologic hypertrophy","Atrophy","Hypotrophy","Fibrinoid necrosis"],"e":"The metaplasia examples include soft tissue (muscle) forming bone after injury, described as pathologic metaplasia.","w":["Hypertrophy is increased cell size.","Atrophy is reduced cell size.","Hypotrophy is developmental under-growth.","Fibrinoid necrosis is in vessel walls."],"k":"Bone in injured soft tissue = pathologic metaplasia.","rn":[["","Example of pathologic metaplasia?","Example of pathologic metaplasia?","Bone in muscle; After injury"]]},{"id":43120,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"Recovery after injury depends on which two factors, according to the summary?","o":["Reversible vs irreversible injury, and the cells' proliferative and differentiation capacity","The patient's blood group and eye colour","The time of day and the season","The organ's size and the patient's age, regardless of the type of injury or cells involved","Only the patient's age"],"e":"The summary states that recovery after injury depends on the type of injury (reversible vs irreversible) and the proliferative and differentiation capacity of the injured cells. The outcome also depends on the type and duration of the stressor.","w":["These are irrelevant.","These are irrelevant.","Organ size is not the key factor.","Age is not the summary's key factor."],"k":"Recovery: injury type + proliferative capacity.","rn":[["","What does cell recovery after injury depend on?","What does cell recovery after injury depend on?","Type of injury; Proliferative capacity; Differentiation capacity"]]},{"id":43121,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"During surgery, blood flow to a patient's kidney is clamped for a period. A biopsy taken just before the clamp is released shows tubular epithelial cells that are swollen, with lipid vacuoles, membrane blebs, increased eosin staining and swollen mitochondria. Most nuclei are still clearly visible, and no apoptotic bodies are seen.<br><br>What is the most likely outcome if blood flow is restored promptly?","o":["Most cells recover, because the injury is still reversible","All cells die, because swelling always means irreversible injury","The cells switch to squamous epithelium by metaplasia within minutes","The tubules liquefy, as in colliquative necrosis of the brain","The cells enter apoptosis because caspases are already active"],"e":"Step 1: swelling (failing energy-dependent ion pumps), lipid vacuoles, increased eosin staining, blebs and swollen mitochondria are signs of injury, but cells can return to normal if the cause is removed in good time. Step 2: visible nuclei and no death mechanisms mean the injury is still reversible; once death processes begin (e.g. caspase activation, nuclear loss), it becomes irreversible.","w":["These are signs of reversible injury, which can recover.","Metaplasia is a slow adaptation via stem cell reprogramming.","Colliquative necrosis is typical of the brain.","No death mechanisms are evident yet."],"k":"Reversible injury recovers if the stressor is removed in time.","rn":[["","When is cell injury reversible?","When is cell injury reversible?","Cell returns to normal; If cause removed in time"],["","Reversible injury: 3 cytoplasm signs?","Reversible injury: 3 cytoplasm signs?","Cell swelling; Lipid vacuoles; More eosinophil staining"]]},{"id":43122,"t":"Cell Death","s":"A 60-year-old with chest pain has a raised blood troponin and creatine kinase. A second patient with a liver disorder has a raised alkaline phosphatase. In both, the injured cells' membranes have broken down.<br><br>Which statement best explains why these blood tests rise?","o":["Necrotic cells leak their contents, so specific proteins appear in blood","Apoptotic bodies release enzymes into the blood after phagocytosis","Autophagy releases digested organelle proteins into the blood","Hypertrophy increases protein synthesis, which spills into the blood","These proteins are made by the recruited inflammatory cells, not by the injured tissue itself"],"e":"Step 1: in necrosis the membrane falls apart and the cytoplasm, including enzymes, leaks into the surrounding tissue. Step 2: this means necrosis can be detected from blood samples by measuring specific proteins: raised troponin or creatine kinase for cardiac muscle necrosis, and raised alkaline phosphatase for liver epithelium necrosis.","w":["Apoptosis has no leakage of cell contents.","Autophagy digests contents within the cell for nutrients.","Hypertrophy is an adaptation, not membrane breakdown.","The proteins come from the dead tissue cells."],"k":"Necrosis leaks contents → measurable blood proteins.","rn":[["","What happens to the cell membrane in necrosis?","What happens to the cell membrane in necrosis?","Falls apart; Cytoplasm and enzymes leak out"],["","Why does a necrotic area stay visible?","Why does a necrotic area stay visible?","Dead cells must be cleared; Contents damage matrix"]]},{"id":43123,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Two tissues are examined after injury. In tissue 1, cells have condensed chromatin and are fragmented into membrane-bound pieces being eaten by phagocytes, with no inflammation. In tissue 2, cells have burst, contents have spilled out, and neutrophils and macrophages have flooded in.<br><br>Which statement correctly compares the two?","o":["Tissue 1: apoptosis, contained; tissue 2: necrosis, with inflammation that can spread damage","Tissue 1: necrosis; tissue 2: apoptosis","Both are apoptosis, as phagocytes are present in each tissue and both sets of cells have fragmented and died","Both are reversible injury that will recover","Tissue 1: autophagy; tissue 2: hypertrophy"],"e":"Step 1: apoptosis is organised: the membrane stays intact, the cell fragments into apoptotic bodies that phagocytes eat, with no leakage and so no inflammation. Step 2: necrosis is disorganised: the membrane falls apart, contents leak, and inflammation (neutrophils, macrophages) follows, which damages more tissue and can spread, even becoming systemic.","w":["The patterns are reversed.","Phagocytes appear in both; bursting and inflammation mean necrosis.","Both show cell death, which is irreversible.","Autophagy is self-digestion; hypertrophy is enlargement."],"k":"Apoptosis: tidy, no inflammation; necrosis: leak + inflammation.","rn":[["","Why does necrosis damage a wider area than the original injury?","Why does necrosis damage a wider area than the original injury?","Inflammation recruits neutrophils and macrophages; They damage more tissue"],["","Why is necrosis bad?","Why is necrosis bad?","Triggers inflammation; Widespread tissue damage"]]},{"id":43124,"t":"Cell Death","s":"A cell receives a dose of radiation that damages its DNA beyond repair. Sensor proteins detect the damage and activate Bax and Bak, which act on the mitochondria. In a second cell with the same damage, high levels of Bcl-2 are present.<br><br>What happens in each cell?","o":["Cell 1 releases cytochrome c and activates caspases; in cell 2 Bcl-2 may block the pathway","Cell 1 undergoes necrosis; cell 2 undergoes apoptosis","Both activate death receptors of the TNF family","Cell 1 enters autophagy; cell 2 becomes hypertrophic","Both cells repair the DNA, because Bax and Bak are protective proteins that block cytochrome c release"],"e":"Step 1: in the intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway, BH3 sensors detect injury such as DNA damage and activate Bcl-2 family effectors (Bax, Bak), causing mitochondria to release cytochrome c, which activates caspases. Step 2: regulators such as Bcl-2 block the effectors; up to caspase activation the pathway can be halted, but once caspases are active it cannot be stopped.","w":["DNA damage most likely leads to apoptosis.","Death receptors belong to the extrinsic pathway.","These are not the described responses.","Bax and Bak promote apoptosis."],"k":"Intrinsic: sensors → Bax/Bak → cytochrome c → caspases; Bcl-2 blocks.","rn":[["","What do BH3 sensors activate?","What do BH3 sensors activate?","Bcl-2 effectors (Bax, Bak); Release cytochrome C"],["","What can stop intrinsic apoptosis if the stressor is removed?","What can stop intrinsic apoptosis if the stressor is removed?","Block proteins (Bcl-2, Bcl-x); Before caspases are active"]]},{"id":43125,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Four autopsy findings are reviewed: (A) a firm area of heart muscle with faint cell outlines and strong pink staining; (B) a liquefied area of brain after hypoxia; (C) chalky areas around the pancreas after acute pancreatitis; (D) a yellow-white, friable lung lesion with a granuloma in tuberculosis.<br><br>Which types of necrosis are shown?","o":["Coagulative; colliquative; fat; caseous","Caseous; coagulative; fibrinoid; colliquative","Colliquative; fat; caseous; coagulative","Fat; colliquative; coagulative; gangrene","Coagulative; fibrinoid; gangrene; fat"],"e":"Step 1: coagulative necrosis preserves architecture with faint outlines, no nuclear stain and strong eosin staining (infarcts); colliquative (liquefactive) necrosis liquefies tissue, as in the brain after hypoxia. Step 2: fat necrosis occurs in acute pancreatitis as leaked lipases attack fat; caseous necrosis is yellow-white and friable, common in TB, with macrophages forming a granuloma.","w":["The order is wrong, and fibrinoid necrosis affects vessel walls.","The order is wrong.","Gangrene is necrosis with putrefaction.","Fibrinoid necrosis affects vessel walls, and gangrene involves putrefaction."],"k":"Heart = coagulative; brain = colliquative; pancreas = fat; TB = caseous.","rn":[["","Necrosis types: 3 common?","Necrosis types: 3 common?","Coagulative; Colliquative; Gangrene"],["","Necrosis types: 3 others?","Necrosis types: 3 others?","Fat; Fibrinoid; Caseous"]]},{"id":43126,"t":"Tissue Organisation","s":"Three patients have tissue damage: patient 1 loses part of the gut lining after an infection; patient 2 has part of the liver removed; patient 3 has a heart attack that kills a region of cardiomyocytes.<br><br>Which prediction about regeneration is most accurate?","o":["Gut regenerates rapidly; liver regrows when stimulated; heart muscle does not regenerate","All three regenerate fully, because all cells can divide","Gut and heart regenerate; liver cannot","None regenerate, because all adult cells are in G0 permanently","Heart regenerates fastest, because cardiomyocytes are labile cells that divide continuously after injury"],"e":"Step 1: gut epithelium is labile, with stem cell pools (crypts) that proliferate continuously; hepatocytes are stable, mostly in G0 but able to restart the cell cycle after damage (so the liver regenerates and part can be donated). Step 2: cardiomyocytes are permanent, terminally differentiated cells in G0 with the cell cycle turned off, so dead heart muscle is not regenerated.","w":["Permanent cells such as cardiomyocytes do not divide.","Cardiomyocytes are permanent; hepatocytes are stable.","G0 is reversible for some cell types, such as hepatocytes.","Cardiomyocytes are terminally differentiated."],"k":"Labile regenerate; stable on demand; permanent never.","rn":[["","Why can hepatocytes regenerate the liver?","Why can hepatocytes regenerate the liver?","Stable cells; Divide on specific stimuli"]]},{"id":43127,"t":"Cell Death","s":"A patient's tissue suffers prolonged nutrient shortage because of restricted blood flow. Biopsy shows membranes from the endoplasmic reticulum wrapping cytoplasm and organelles, which then fuse with lysosomes. The stressor continues for weeks.<br><br>What process is occurring, and what may follow if the stressor is not removed?","o":["Autophagy, which may proceed to apoptosis","Phagocytosis of bacteria, which leads to hyperplasia","Metaplasia, which may proceed to necrosis","Hypertrophy, which may proceed to autophagy","Apoptosis, which may reverse into autophagy"],"e":"Step 1: autophagy is self-eating in response to lack of nutrients: autophagosomes form from ER membranes, engulf cytoplasm and organelles, fuse with lysosomes, and the digested contents supply nutrients (it can occur when blood flow is restricted). Step 2: it can be protective, but if the cell has eaten too much of itself or the stressor is not removed, it can be followed by apoptosis.","w":["The cell is digesting itself, not bacteria.","Metaplasia is cell-type replacement via stem cells.","Hypertrophy is enlargement, not self-digestion.","Once caspases are active, apoptosis cannot be reversed."],"k":"Autophagy (nutrient lack) → may lead to apoptosis.","rn":[["","When does autophagy lead to apoptosis?","When does autophagy lead to apoptosis?","Stressor not removed; Too many organelles eliminated"],["","Is autophagy protective?","Is autophagy protective?","Can be; Or leads to apoptosis; If excessive"]]},{"id":43128,"t":"Cell Death","s":"Three cells are exposed to different insults: cell A is deprived of oxygen and its ATP falls; cell B accumulates misfolded proteins in its endoplasmic reticulum; cell C is exposed to radiation that damages its DNA.<br><br>What is each cell's most likely fate?","o":["A: injury then necrosis; B: apoptosis; C: apoptosis","A: apoptosis; B: necrosis; C: necrosis","A: hypertrophy; B: hyperplasia; C: metaplasia","A: apoptosis; B: autophagy; C: necrosis","All three: necrosis with inflammation"],"e":"Step 1: hypoxia/ischaemia reduces ATP, so energy-dependent functions fail, causing cell injury and then necrosis. Step 2: accumulation of misfolded proteins in the ER and DNA damage from radiation most likely lead to apoptosis. The lecturer stressed this is about probability, not black and white.","w":["Oxygen lack typically leads to necrosis.","These are adaptations, not responses to these insults.","The outcomes are mismatched.","Misfolded proteins and DNA damage most likely cause apoptosis."],"k":"↓ATP → necrosis; misfolded protein or DNA damage → apoptosis.","rn":[["","Does a given injury always cause the same cell death?","Does a given injury always cause the same cell death?","No; it is a probability; e.g. infection: necrosis or apoptosis"],["","What are the 2 possible outcomes of irreversible cell injury?","What are the 2 possible outcomes of irreversible cell injury?","Apoptosis; Necrosis"],["","Why is apoptosis protective after injury?","Why is apoptosis protective after injury?","Prevents injury progressing to necrosis; Avoids inflammation"]]},{"id":43129,"t":"Cell Growth","s":"Researchers take terminally differentiated cells from a tissue and grow them in culture. They divide a couple of times at most and then die out. Cells from the same tissue's stem cell pool continue to divide and produce new differentiated cells.<br><br>Which explanation is correct?","o":["Differentiated cells sit in G0 and lose proliferation; stem cells keep it","Differentiated cells are stuck in M phase and cannot finish dividing, while stem cells rest in G0","Stem cells are in G0 permanently and cannot divide","Differentiated cells can turn back into stem cells to divide","All adult cells divide indefinitely in culture"],"e":"Step 1: terminally differentiated cells stay in an extended G1 called G0 (resting) and lose the ability to proliferate; in culture they divide a couple of times at most, then die out. Step 2: stem and progenitor cells keep the ability to proliferate, coupling division with differentiation; the process is one-way, so a progenitor cannot go back to a stem cell.","w":["Terminally differentiated cells rest in G0, not M phase.","Stem cells keep the ability to proliferate.","Differentiation is one-way.","Differentiated cells divide only a couple of times."],"k":"Differentiated = G0, no proliferation; stem cells keep dividing.","rn":[["","What lies between a stem cell and a mature cell?","What lies between a stem cell and a mature cell?","Intermediate differentiated progenitor cells"],["","G0","G0","Extended G1; Terminally differentiated cells; Cycle no longer progresses"],["","Do differentiated cells change type in metaplasia?","Do differentiated cells change type in metaplasia?","No; Stem cells reprogrammed"]]},{"id":43130,"t":"Cell Injury","s":"A patient's blocked leg artery is reopened after several hours. Although oxygenated blood now flows again, the muscle shows new cell stress and inflammation over the next day.<br><br>Which explanation best fits?","o":["Reperfusion itself causes cell stress and inflammation after ischaemia","Restored blood flow always reverses all injury immediately","The muscle cells have become stable cells that divide rapidly","The inflammation is apoptosis, which always causes inflammation","Oxygen causes hyperplasia of the muscle, which looks like inflammation"],"e":"Step 1: ischaemia is restriction of blood flow leading to oxygen starvation, a cause of cell injury. Step 2: reperfusion, the return of oxygenated blood after a period of restriction, itself causes cell stress and inflammation (ischaemia-reperfusion injury).","w":["Reperfusion can itself cause injury.","Skeletal muscle cells are permanent and do not divide.","Apoptosis causes no inflammation.","Skeletal muscle does not respond by hyperplasia."],"k":"Reperfusion can add stress and inflammation.","rn":[["","What does reperfusion cause?","What does reperfusion cause?","Cell stress; Inflammation"]]}]);
